window.DESIGN_STUDIO={"model":"claude-opus-5-5","subjects":[{"id":"site","name":"Overall site","kind":"site","expected":4,"recommended":"B","diagnosis":"James asked for designs that follow the main page's theme. B changes the least about how the site looks: the same paper, cards, wordmark, fonts and green. It changes what is wrong with the structure: duplicated promotion, provider filters, and favorites and profile buried in disclosures. All four directions share one home style, so the choice is about layout, not brand.","designs":[{"id":"A","name":"Ledger","rationale":"Editorial and typographic. Everything sits directly on the home paper with hairline rules, and nothing is boxed except the home's tilted daily-edition card, which here becomes the 'Your day' counter. Hierarchy comes from DM Serif scale and order. It is the calmest page and the shortest, and it shows every game once.","layoutStrategy":"Home masthead (brand left, text-link anchors, profile button) → hero: eyebrow, two-line DM Serif date with the month in green italic, plus the rotated 'Your day' card holding the finished count and Share → Today's Three as three full-width ruled rows (serif numeral, home icon, category eyebrow, serif name, description, status, Play ↗, ☆ Favorite, Hide) → collection index on paper with segment, kind chips, a descriptions toggle and search, grouped into two columns of ruled single entries → two-column colophon for feedback and profile, split by a hairline → home footer.","typography":"DM Serif 64px date, 32px section titles, 30px Today names, 22px index names. DM Sans 15–16px body, 14px descriptions, 11px eyebrows.","spacing":"Home page-shell width (1120px, 32px side margins; 14px on phone). Rows 24px vertical padding. Sections separated by 64px and a 1px rule; Today's list opens with a 1px ink rule.","palette":"Shared tokens only. Paper is visible across the whole page; the only warm-white surfaces are the day card, the popover and the form fields.","depthMaterial":"Flattest of the four: hairlines only, plus the home's 0 6px 0 shadow on the day card and its 2° tilt, which is the one playful note borrowed from the home hero.","motion":"None beyond hover underlines. The day card's tilt is removed under reduced motion.","accessibility":"Real list semantics and heading order. Favorite and Hide are aria-pressed buttons with visible text. The filter is a radiogroup with counts. '/' focuses search (wired). The date is a <time> element.","navigation":"Text-link anchors in the masthead (Today, Collection, Favorites, Hidden, Feedback). On phone they become one horizontally scrolling row, with the profile icon button beside the brand.","resultSharing":"'Share today's result' ink pill in the day card, disabled until the first finish. Preview text gives game, metric and ✓ only.","recommendationTradeoff":"Fastest to scan and cheapest to build, and very close to the home's own editorial voice. Trade-off: the least tactile direction, and long index rows read as a list rather than a collection. Best kept as B's List mode.","title":"Ledger","summary":"Editorial and typographic. Everything sits directly on the home paper with hairline rules, and nothing is boxed except the home's tilted daily-edition card, which here becomes the 'Your day' counter. Hierarchy comes from DM Serif scale and order. It is the calmest page and the shortest, and it shows every game once.","path":"director/site-A.html"},{"id":"B","name":"Tray","rationale":"The live home's own cards, de-duplicated and given one quiet container. Today's Three are three standard home game cards seated in a #eeeee6 well (the home's daily-progress panel colour), so 'today' reads as a set without any new material. The collection is one grid of the same cards, each shown once, with Favorite and Hide in the familiar card footer. A returning player sees the site they know, minus the repetition.","layoutStrategy":"Home masthead (brand, Feedback text link, profile button) → eyebrow and DM Serif 'Today's Three, ready when you are.' with the green italic phrase, date line, finished count and Share pill → tray of three home cards (category eyebrow plus status dot, icon and serif name, description, paper slot with the real metric and score marks, footer with Play ↗, ☆ Favorite, Hide) → collection: 'Keep your favorites close.' with the All/Favorites/Hidden segment, kind chips, a Cards/List switch and search, then a card grid (four columns at 1440, one at 393) with List mode as one ruled card → a well holding two cards for feedback and profile → home footer.","typography":"DM Serif 56px heading, 32px Today names, 25px collection names. DM Sans 15px descriptions, 14px move lines, 12–13px controls.","spacing":"Home 1120px shell. Tray 14px padding and 14px gaps; cards 22px padding; collection gaps 16px.","palette":"Shared tokens plus the #eeeee6 well. Category eyebrows use each game's home identity colour.","depthMaterial":"The home card exactly: warm-white face, 1px line, 22px radius, 0 6px 0 #202c3505 at rest, and a 3px lift with the home's soft hover shadow. Score marks reuse the home progress-mark squares. No inset or keycap effects.","motion":"Cards lift 3px on hover in 180ms, as on the home. Nothing else moves. Reduced motion removes the lift.","accessibility":"The segment is a radiogroup with wired Left/Right arrow keys and a roving tabindex. Toggles carry text. The Cards/List switch uses aria-pressed. The status dot is always paired with a word.","navigation":"Minimal, like the home: brand, a Feedback text link and a profile button. The collection has inline search. Favorites and Hidden are first-class segment positions with counts.","resultSharing":"Each finished card's slot shows the metric and filled marks. 'Share today's result' composes all three as text with no answers, and is locked until the first finish.","recommendationTradeoff":"RECOMMENDED. It is the smallest visual step from the live home and fixes its real problems (each game shown up to three times, promo banners, the provider filter, profile and favorites hidden in disclosures) without a new look. It also gives game sessions a direct template: a home card is the unit, the well groups related cards, and green means act or done. Trade-off: it is the least novel, and 33 cards still form a grid. Use the List mode and the strict one-card-per-game rule to keep it from becoming the old repeated grid.","title":"Tray","summary":"The live home's own cards, de-duplicated and given one quiet container. Today's Three are three standard home game cards seated in a #eeeee6 well (the home's daily-progress panel colour), so 'today' reads as a set without any new material. The collection is one grid of the same cards, each shown once, with Favorite and Hide in the familiar card footer. A returning player sees the site they know, minus the repetition.","path":"director/site-B.html"},{"id":"C","name":"Console","rationale":"For the returning daily player: a persistent rail, a main column and a detail card that explains any selected game in three numbered lines next to its Play, Favorite and Hide actions. Rules appear where you choose. It is built only from home parts: paper rail, home cards and the ink segment.","layoutStrategy":"Desktop: a 248px rail on paper, divided by a hairline (brand, Today, Collection, Favorites, Hidden with counts, How it plays with four kind markers, then Feedback and Profile) | main: DM Serif date heading with search, Today's Three as three connected home cards with numbered pins, then the collection table inside one home card | a 372px sticky detail card. Below 1240px the detail card slides in from the right. Below 860px the rail becomes a five-tab bottom bar and the detail card a bottom sheet.","typography":"DM Serif 50px date, 29px section titles, 34px detail title, 21px table names. DM Sans 13–14px table text, eyebrow column headers.","spacing":"8px grid. 44px rail items, about 64px table rows. The detail card keeps 24px of paper around it, so it never reads as a full-height panel.","palette":"Shared tokens. Selected table row uses the green wash; the selected Today card has a 2px green border. Kind markers use home colours: clay, green, ink and the home emblem gold.","depthMaterial":"Flat paper rail, home cards for stops, table and detail. The only overlay shadow is a soft home-dialog shadow on the narrow-screen drawer, with the home's ink scrim.","motion":"The drawer slides in 200ms; cards lift as on the home. Reduced motion makes both instant.","accessibility":"Landmarks: nav (rail), main, complementary (detail). Real <table> with headers. Each game name is a button, so rows are keyboard reachable. Wired shortcuts: / search, J/K move the selection, Enter opens details (when focus is not on a button or link), F favorite, H hide, Esc closes. No shortcut navigates away.","navigation":"Always visible. Favorites and Hidden are rail destinations with live counts.","resultSharing":"'Share today's result' sits in the Today's Three header. The detail card shows the selected game's metric state.","recommendationTradeoff":"Best for rule clarity and keyboard play. Trade-off: the most app-like, it is heavy for a first phone visit, and a table feels administrative next to the home's warmth. Borrow its three-line rule summary for a game detail sheet even if it isn't chosen.","title":"Console","summary":"For the returning daily player: a persistent rail, a main column and a detail card that explains any selected game in three numbered lines next to its Play, Favorite and Hide actions. Rules appear where you choose. It is built only from home parts: paper rail, home cards and the ink segment.","path":"director/site-C.html"},{"id":"D","name":"Specimen","rationale":"Show the play, not a pictogram. Each of Today's Three is a home card whose top half is a paper inset holding the puzzle's real opening state (Shortlist's twelve words, Second Sense's noun and verb clues, Sideways' strips). The collection leads every card with the move you make. It answers the generic-card problem while staying in the home's card language.","layoutStrategy":"Home masthead with text-link anchors and profile → 'Today's Three, at a glance.' with count and Share → three specimen cards (paper inset miniature labelled 'Opening state', category eyebrow, icon and serif name, bold move line, description, then status, Play ↗, ☆ Favorite, Hide) → collection grouped by how it plays, three cards across with icon, serif name, category and a '→ move' line, plus a sticky filter row on desktop → a feedback card with a paper explainer half and the form → home footer.","typography":"DM Serif 58px heading, 32px specimen names, 24px catalogue names. Board letters in miniatures are DM Sans bold at 14–17px.","spacing":"1200px shell. Specimens three across at 1440 and stacked on phone, with the miniature beside the copy between 760px and 1100px.","palette":"Shared tokens. Miniatures keep each game's local board semantics (Second Sense noun/verb hues; Sideways column guides in green).","depthMaterial":"Home cards outside; inside, the miniature tiles have a 2px flat bottom edge in the line colour, the same subtle tactility as the home and game tiles. No drop shadows on tiles.","motion":"On hover or focus a specimen plays one true-to-rules beat once (Shortlist's scan bar sweeps; one Sideways strip nudges a column). Disabled under reduced motion.","accessibility":"Miniatures are aria-hidden, with a text equivalent. Real controls sit in the card footer. Search also matches move lines.","navigation":"Masthead anchors (Today, Collection, Favorites, Hidden, Feedback) plus a profile button. A sticky filter row on desktop.","resultSharing":"A finished specimen swaps its miniature for score-only marks (green ✓ squares), never letters. 'Share today's result' sits in the heading row.","recommendationTradeoff":"The most distinctive, and the best at teaching mechanics at a glance. Trade-off: it needs a per-game miniature renderer fed by each day's data, kept spoiler-free. Viable for Today's Three; the catalogue uses the move line instead.","title":"Specimen","summary":"Show the play, not a pictogram. Each of Today's Three is a home card whose top half is a paper inset holding the puzzle's real opening state (Shortlist's twelve words, Second Sense's noun and verb clues, Sideways' strips). The collection leads every card with the move you make. It answers the generic-card problem while staying in the home's card language.","path":"director/site-D.html"}]},{"id":"ringcount","name":"Ringcount","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"James called it 'pretty easy and boring'. The live game has twelve serif numerals on a pale ring with twelve dashed '+' gap buttons outside it: 24 circular targets of similar weight, so the actual decision (where to cut) looks no more important than the numbers. On phones it is unclear which two numbers a cut separates. The hub states the target sum, and the solve becomes quick arithmetic. It uses the older cream template.","clarityRisk":"Low for rules. Medium for the cut targets on phones.","review":{"slug":"ringcount","name":"Ringcount","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Twelve serif numerals on a pale ring plus twelve dashed '+' gap buttons outside the ring: 24 circular targets of similar weight, so the actual decision (where to cut) looks no more important than the numbers. The 'EACH PART 16' hub hands over the key number, and the solve becomes quick arithmetic, which matches 'easy and boring'. It uses the older cream template with an italic serif tagline and a 'Try the bonus challenge' bar.","clarityRisk":"Low for rules. On phone, gap buttons outside the band make it unclear which two numbers a cut separates.","bestDesignOpportunity":"One direction only: a segmented machined band where the gaps are physical slots inside the ring between numbers, and each completed arc shows its running sum along its length. Optional mechanical experiment (separate from UI): a bonus ring that adds a second constraint (for example equal arc lengths for two parts) to answer 'easy'. Don't spend keeper-level effort.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A — Cut Ring, the only direction for this replacement candidate. The UI alone cannot fix 'boring'; the separately labelled Sealed-totals experiment is the lever for that.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Cut Ring","summary":"A dial with a thick warm-grey band. Each arc between cuts takes one of four live identity accents (Switchpath blue, Inbetween teal, Ringcount ochre, Counterpart clay; revision 2 dropped the old navy) and shows its running sum inside the ring, in green when it reaches 16. Gap dots sit just outside the band between numbers and become ink cut marks when placed. The centre reads 'EACH ARC 16 · 3 of 4 cuts'. A side list repeats each arc as an addition (e.g. 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16 ✓).","layout":"On desktop the 460px dial sits beside a 320px panel (arc list, Hint, Check circle, experiment box). On phones it stacks.","interaction":"Tap a gap dot to cut or uncut (up to four). Tap an arc in the list to highlight it on the ring and dim the others. Check evaluates the real 12-number sample locally. The 'Sealed totals' checkbox hides sums until Check.","motion":"Gap dots grow on hover. A placed cut becomes a rotated bar. Unfocused arcs fade to 25%.","accessibility":"Gap hit areas are 44px buttons labelled 'Place cut between 4 and 5'. Numbers are labelled by position. Arc sums appear as text in the list, not only as ring colour.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: checks and hints used, solved or not, no cut positions.","tradeoff":"Makes cutting the main decision and shows sums where they apply. Keeping the target '16' in the centre preserves the current rules but also the ease. Sealed totals is a separate mechanic experiment (three checks), not part of the current rules.","mockup":"ringcount-A.html","path":"team-01/ringcount-A.html"}]},{"id":"switchpath","name":"Switchpath","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The live game already has the clearest direction cue on the site: a solid blue 'GO HIGHER' banner. But the banner sits above the board, so on a phone the eye shuttles between it and the cell being chosen. The END cell uses a beige fill with a brown outline, which breaks the no-brown rule. Links between visited cells are faint dotted lines. Values 1–16 are spread so widely that most comparisons are trivial. James asked for clearer high/low indicators during play, tighter numbers and a possible 5×5. He approved blue = higher and teal = lower.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The requirement flips after every step, so it has to be visible where the next tap happens, not only in a header.","review":{"slug":"switchpath","name":"Switchpath","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"It has the clearest direction cue on the site (a solid blue 'GO HIGHER · Greater than 4' banner) and a legible 4×4 grid with START/END labels. Problems: the cue sits above the board, so on phone the eye shuttles between banner and cell; the END cell uses a beige fill with a brown outline, which sits outside both the approved blue/teal board semantics and the home's ink/green chrome; links between visited cells are faint dotted lines; numbers 1–16 are spread so widely that comparisons are trivial.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The requirement alternates every step, so it must be visible at the point of the next tap.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Put the requirement on the board. The current cell carries a blue up-chevron (higher) or teal down-chevron (lower), and legal neighbours get a matching rim while illegal ones stay neutral. The path becomes a continuous engraved channel whose segments alternate blue/teal. Keep a compact banner as a restatement. Replace the brown END with an ink outline and flag glyph. Optional, separate: a 5×5 bonus board with tighter number spacing, as a harder bonus round rather than a replacement daily.","priority":"high"},"recommended":"A — Signal Board. The rule colour physically wraps the board, and legal neighbours carry a matching outline and ↑/↓ badge, so the rule is visible at the point of the tap. Add C's arrow-key handling (A lists keys but doesn't wire them) and D's share card. Closer numbers and a 5×5 board are content/mechanic changes that none of the four concepts implements; treat them as a separate optional bonus experiment.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Signal Board","summary":"The 4×4 board sits inside a frame that is solid blue when the next step must go higher and teal when it must go lower. Its header reads 'GO HIGHER / Greater than 3' with a large ↑/↓ glyph. Legal neighbours get a white face, a rule-coloured outline and a small ↑/↓ badge. The route is drawn as alternating blue/teal segments.","layout":"On desktop, the board (max 440px) sits to the left of a 300px side column holding 'Your rhythm' (the visited values joined by ↑/↓ chips, ending in a dashed '?'), a moves/hints/perfect tally and a collapsible Rules & keys panel. On phones it is a single column: board, then Undo / Reset path / Hint, a cost line, and a live message.","interaction":"Tap an outlined neighbour to extend the route, or tap an earlier step to rewind (+1 move). Illegal taps shake and explain why without spending a move: not adjacent, wrong direction, or END before the 16th cell. Undo and Reset add +1 move. Hint only shows a 'not wired' message.","motion":"The frame colour cross-fades over 0.3s when the direction flips. The current cell scales slightly. Illegal cells shake for 0.28s.","accessibility":"Cells are native buttons labelled with value, start/end, step number and 'legal next step'. The direction header is aria-live. Direction is carried by glyph and words ('GO LOWER'), not only colour. Cells are reachable with Tab and Enter. The rules panel now says the proposed arrow-key and Backspace shortcuts are not wired in this prototype.","sharing":"Not shown in this mockup. Use D's card: moves, hints, 16/16 connected, never the route.","tradeoff":"Has the strongest at-the-tap cue, but the full-colour frame is a large saturated area on a light page. Outlining legal neighbours also removes some of the scanning work, which may make an already easy game feel easier.","mockup":"switchpath-A.html","path":"team-01/switchpath-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Rhythm Line","summary":"Adds a plotted trace above the board: each visited value is a point on a 1–16 vertical scale, joined by blue (up) and teal (down) segments. A shaded band marks where the next number must fall. The board itself is a plain hairline grid with the current cell in solid ink.","layout":"On desktop the board is on the left (≤400px) with the trace panel, a moves/hints/perfect fact strip and a live message to its right. On phones the trace comes first and the board follows.","interaction":"Hovering or focusing a cell draws a dashed ghost segment to where that value would land on the line. It is drawn red if the value falls outside the band. Tapping commits or rewinds. Undo, Reset and Hint work as in A.","motion":"The ghost segment redraws on hover and focus. Illegal cells shake.","accessibility":"The trace is role=img with an aria-label. Focusing a cell with Tab triggers the same preview as hover. Step numbers carry a small coloured dot plus the number.","sharing":"Not shown. A dataless rhythm line (shape only, no values) could be a share image, but it could hint at the route on a fixed board, so plain counts are safer.","tradeoff":"Makes the 'higher then lower' rhythm visible and teaches the rule well, but it puts the main cue in a second panel away from the board, which is the shuttling problem the live game already has.","mockup":"switchpath-B.html","path":"team-01/switchpath-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Compass Pad","summary":"Beside a ruled 4×4 board, a 3×3 pad shows only your four neighbours as raised keys (UP / LEFT / RIGHT / DOWN). Each key shows the neighbour's value and its signed difference (+4, −2). The centre is a round blue or teal core with the current number and 'GO HIGHER' or 'GO LOWER'.","layout":"On desktop, the board and pad sit side by side (420px + 340px). On phones they stack, with board above pad. Edge and visited neighbours appear as disabled keys labelled 'edge' or 'visited'.","interaction":"Move by tapping a pad key, tapping the board, or pressing the arrow keys. Backspace undoes. Keys that fit the rule have a filled difference chip; keys that don't fit show the chip in red wash.","motion":"Pad keys travel down 2px with a thinning bottom edge when pressed. Illegal moves shake.","accessibility":"This is the only Switchpath concept with arrow-key and Backspace handling wired. Pad keys are 76px tall and speak, e.g., 'UP: 13, +2, fits'. Fit is shown by the signed number and the words, not only colour.","sharing":"Not shown. Same counts as A.","tradeoff":"This is the best keyboard and phone input of the four, and the difference numbers make 'higher/lower' arithmetic explicit. But the pad does the comparison for you, which lowers difficulty, and it splits attention between pad and board.","mockup":"switchpath-C.html","path":"team-01/switchpath-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Ink Route & Switch","summary":"A paper sheet with a dot grid and serif numerals, where the route is a thick ink stroke alternating blue and teal. A two-position HIGHER/LOWER switch sits beside the sheet (vertical on desktop, horizontal on phones), with 'than 15' beneath it. The side column includes a share-card preview.","layout":"On desktop there are three columns: a 92px switch, the sheet (≤440px) and a 260px side with Undo/Reset/Hint, a tally and a share-card toggle. On phones it stacks: switch, sheet, controls.","interaction":"Hovering a neighbour sketches a dashed stroke, blue/teal if legal or red if it would break the switch. Tap to commit; tap an earlier step to rewind. 'Preview share card' expands an illustrative card.","motion":"The switch lights the active half with a 0.3s colour change. The ghost stroke follows the hover. Illegal taps shake.","accessibility":"The switch is role=status, aria-live. Stroke previews are pointer-hover only, with no focus equivalent in this prototype. Cells are labelled buttons.","sharing":"Shown: 'Switchpath · No. 018 · Perfect route · 16/16 connected · 15 moves · 0 hints · Route never shown', marked as illustrative.","tradeoff":"The most refined, least gamey look, and the only concept with a share card. But the direction cue sits beside the board rather than on it, and the serif numerals on a dotted sheet lean editorial rather than physical.","mockup":"switchpath-D.html","path":"team-01/switchpath-D.html"}]},{"id":"counterpart","name":"Counterpart","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The live game has a word bank of ten small uppercase pills above five relationship rows (THESIS → ARGUMENT, PRISM → SPECTRUM…). The active row has dashed 'First/Second word' slots and small Reverse/Hint/Check controls. Type is 16–18px and James explicitly asked for larger. Direction is the core rule, but it is stated in a small 'Direction matters →' caption, and Reverse is a separate button rather than a property of the pair. It uses the older cream template.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Getting the direction wrong is the main failure, and the current UI does not make direction geometric.","review":{"slug":"counterpart","name":"Counterpart","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A teal word-bank panel of ten uppercase pills (HIGH, SOLDIER, PROOF, KILN…), then five relationship rows (THESIS → ARGUMENT, PRISM → SPECTRUM…). The current row holds dashed 'First word / Second word' slots plus small Reverse, Hint and Check pair controls. The other rows show 'choose row', which wraps to two lines on phone. Type is 16–18px; James explicitly wants larger. It uses the cream older template.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Direction is the core rule, but 'Direction matters →' is a small right-aligned caption, and Reverse is a separate button rather than a property of the pair.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Make the relationship the hero: the example pair at 28–32px with a physical arrow bar between the words, and the answer pair below mirroring the same bar so direction is geometry. The bank becomes a large 22px two-column list. Selected words drop into slots, and Reverse rotates the arrow bar. Only the active row is expanded; solved rows collapse into a compact ledger.","priority":"high"},"recommended":"B — Relationship Bridge. The given pair and your pair are stacked as two parallel arrows with 'same role, same side' guides between them, so direction becomes geometry, and the words are the largest type in the set. Add D's number-key shortcuts and A's compact ledger as the solved-rows overview.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Analogy Ledger","summary":"All five relationships are ruled ledger lines (01–05) with the cue pair at 20–26px, a serif 'as' and two answer slots joined by a blue arrow. A sticky bottom tray holds the bank and Reverse / Hint / Check pair. Solved rows turn green and show a one-line reason (wording marked illustrative).","layout":"Single centred column (max 820px). On desktop each line reads cue | as | answer in one row; on phones the cue sits above the answer slots. The tray sticks to the bottom of the viewport.","interaction":"Tap a line or slot to make it active, then tap bank words to fill first and second. Tapping a placed word removes it. Reverse swaps the active pair. Check and Hint only show 'not wired' messages.","motion":"Minimal: a blue bar marks the active line. No animation beyond state changes.","accessibility":"Slots are buttons labelled 'Row 1 second word: empty'. Used words are disabled and struck through. No keyboard shortcuts.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: checks and hints used, five solved marks, no words.","tradeoff":"Gives the best overview of all five rows and the sticky tray suits phones. But direction is still only an arrow glyph in a row, and the type is smaller than B or D.","mockup":"counterpart-A.html","path":"team-01/counterpart-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Relationship Bridge","summary":"One relationship at a time on a white plate. The given pair (e.g. BASS → LOW) is set at up to 34px with an ink arrow bar between the words. Directly beneath, your pair sits in two large slots joined by a blue arrow bar, with dashed vertical guides labelled 'same role, same side'. A five-step rail above shows open, draft and paired rows.","layout":"On desktop the bridge plate sits beside a 330px two-column word bank. On phones the bank follows the plate. Reverse, 'Name the relationship' (hint) and Check pair sit under the plate.","interaction":"Choose a row from the step rail, then tap bank words into the highlighted slot. Bank words show a '1st'/'2nd' tag once placed. Reverse animates the two slots crossing before they swap. After a hint, the arrow label is meant to show the relationship name; in the solved row it shows 'protects ↦'.","motion":"On Reverse the two slots slide 30% toward each other with a springy ease, then swap. This makes direction a physical property of the pair.","accessibility":"Rail buttons speak 'Relationship 4: BASS to LOW'. Bank words are 52px tall in a two-column grid. Messages are aria-live. There are no keyboard shortcuts in this prototype.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: checks and hints, five solved marks, no words.","tradeoff":"Has the largest words and makes direction geometric, which answers James's readability request directly. But only one row is visible at a time, so cross-row elimination needs the step rail.","mockup":"counterpart-B.html","path":"team-01/counterpart-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Workbench","summary":"A two-column table ('Given relationship' | 'Your counterpart · same direction') with a per-row Check button. The bank is a column of physical labels with a grip texture. A lifted label tilts and raises, and every open space turns blue.","layout":"On desktop the bank is a sticky 240px column on the left of the table. On phones the bank wraps above, and table rows become single-column.","interaction":"Drag a label onto any space with the mouse (HTML drag-and-drop), or tap a label then tap a space. Tap a placed word to return it, or press Esc to drop the lifted label. Each row has its own Check.","motion":"The lifted label rises 3px and rotates −1.2° with a deeper shadow. The drop target fills solid blue on drag-over.","accessibility":"Spaces are buttons labelled with row and position. Tap-to-place is the touch path, because HTML drag-and-drop does not work on most touch browsers. Esc is wired.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as B.","tradeoff":"This is the most tactile, and it lets players fill any row in any order. But the table is dense on phones, and per-row Check buttons add five more controls.","mockup":"counterpart-C.html","path":"team-01/counterpart-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Sentence Focus","summary":"Each analogy is a large serif sentence: 'PRISM is to SPECTRUM as ___ is to ___'. The blanks are underlined inline buttons. A line below states which role each blank plays. Bank words are numbered keys (1–0) in a two-column grid (five columns on wider screens).","layout":"Single focused column (max 860px). A five-segment progress rail sits at top, then the sentence at 24–46px, the role line, the keys, Reverse / Hint / Check, and Previous/Next.","interaction":"Tap or press 1–0 to fill the current blank, R to reverse, Backspace to clear, ←/→ to change analogy, and Enter to check (message only).","motion":"None beyond state changes. The current blank gets a blue underline and wash.","accessibility":"This is the strongest keyboard path of the four, with all shortcuts wired. The sentence is aria-live, and each key shows its number in a kbd chip.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as B.","tradeoff":"Reading the analogy aloud makes direction natural, and keyboard play is fast. But the sentence form is wordy for long words on phones, and it is the least physical concept.","mockup":"counterpart-D.html","path":"team-01/counterpart-D.html"}]},{"id":"shortlist","name":"Shortlist","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The live game shows twelve words in a card grid, then 'Choose scan letters', then a dark navy scan panel with a 26-key alphabet. The scan panel is the heart of the game, but it is in a different (dark) material and below the fold on phones. 'Pencil mode: OFF' and tap-to-final-pick are hidden modes in a one-shot game.","clarityRisk":"High. There are three scans and one irreversible pick. Players need their evidence beside the words, and the final pick must be unmistakable.","review":{"slug":"shortlist","name":"Shortlist","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Twelve words in a 3×4 card grid, then 'Choose scan letters', then a dark navy scan panel with a 26-key alphabet and '? + ?' slots, then 'Words & results ↑'. The scan panel is the heart of the game, but it is in a different (dark) material and below the fold on phone. 'Pencil mode: OFF' and tap-to-final-pick are hidden modes in a one-shot game.","clarityRisk":"High. With three scans and one irreversible pick, players need to see which words each scan eliminated next to the words themselves, and the pick action must be unmistakable.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Keep words and evidence together. Each scan adds a column of marks beside every word (consistent / ruled out), so the word list becomes an evidence ledger. The two-slot scan composer is pinned under the list, with the alphabet as a light sheet. Make 'Final pick' an explicit latched mode rather than tap-to-confirm. Pencil eliminations become a physical strike latch.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Pinned Evidence. Scan results stay pinned above the list, every word has its own cross-off control, and the final pick is a separate confirm bar with a 'Not yet' escape. Offer C's sorting trays as an optional Practice tool and D's growing type as a motion detail.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Pinned Evidence","summary":"A sticky strip of three scan cards stays at the top: SCAN 1 'A+E → 1' (illustrative), SCAN 2 marked NEXT in blue, and SCAN 3 dashed. Each word tile is split into a pick area and a ✕ cross-off button. A white dock below holds the '? + ?' composer, a QWERTY keyboard and a strategy hint.","layout":"Single column (max 760px). The evidence strip is sticky, then a count line ('6 on your list'), a 2- or 3-column word grid, an ink confirm bar, and the composer dock.","interaction":"✕ crosses a word off or restores it. Tapping a word opens 'Is it SAINT? You still have 2 scans. One pick ends the round' with 'Not yet' and 'Final pick'. Choose two letters by keyboard or typing, then Scan (message only). Esc clears.","motion":"The confirm bar appears in place. No other animation.","accessibility":"Pick and cross-off are separate buttons with explicit labels ('Cross off CRISP', 'SAINT, choose as final pick'). Letters scanned in scan 1 are greyed and labelled. Typing letters is wired.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: scans used (n/3), hint used or not, found or missed, no letters or words.","tradeoff":"This is the clearest one-shot pick flow and keeps evidence in view while scrolling. The ✕ marks are the player's own notes, not deductions, which the copy states. The board stays a grid of tiles, so it is the least novel of the four.","mockup":"shortlist-A.html","path":"team-01/shortlist-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Letter Lens","summary":"Each word is spelled in five small letter cells. With the 'Letter lens' toggle on, the two letters you are about to scan light up inside every word: first letter blue, second letter teal. A side panel holds the evidence list, the scan composer with an A–Z grid and a 'Final pick…' sheet with radio buttons.","layout":"On desktop the word case is on the left with a 340px evidence/compose panel on the right. On phones the panel follows the words.","interaction":"Tap a word to cross it off or restore it. Pick letters by the A–Z grid or by typing. Toggle the lens. 'Final pick…' expands a radio list of remaining words with a confirm button (message only).","motion":"The lens toggle slides. Letter cells fill instantly.","accessibility":"The toggle uses aria-pressed. The lens uses two colours plus position (first/second), and the composer repeats the letters as text. Typing is wired.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"The lens saves letter-hunting and makes a scan's reach visible. But it is an assist, so the footer proposes it off by default in the Daily. The final pick sits behind a sheet, which is safe but one step further away.","mockup":"shortlist-B.html","path":"team-01/shortlist-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Sorting Trays","summary":"Each scan is a tab ('SCAN 1 · RESULT 1 · A + E'). Under it, three trays are labelled 0 neither, 1 exactly one, 2 both. The player sorts words into trays by hand, and the tray matching the scan result is marked KEEP while the others dim and strike through.","layout":"Single column (max 900px): scan tabs, an unsorted pool, three side-by-side trays, and a composer that appears on the scan-2 tab. Strategy hint and 'Final pick…' sit at the bottom.","interaction":"Tap a word, then tap a tray. Nothing is checked automatically, and the copy says so. The scan-2 tab only offers words kept from scan 1. Letters come from the A–Z grid. Scan and Final pick show messages only.","motion":"Trays fade to 50% when they are not the kept tray.","accessibility":"Trays are role=button with tabindex. Tabs use role=tab and aria-selected. There are no keyboard shortcuts for sorting.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"This is the most faithful to how the deduction actually works, and it replaces Pencil mode with something meaningful. But it is the most work per scan, three trays are tight at 320px, and sorting is manual. Better suited to Practice or as an optional tool.","mockup":"shortlist-C.html","path":"team-01/shortlist-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"The Shelf","summary":"Remaining words are set as large free-flowing type, and the type grows as the list shrinks (22px at nine or more words, up to 46px at two). Set-aside words drop to a small shelf below. A dashed 'Final pick' slot has to be filled and then confirmed. Scans are a numbered timeline in a left column.","layout":"On desktop a 220px timeline and composer sit on the left of the word field. On phones the timeline becomes three small cards above.","interaction":"Scan letters are typed into two native single-letter inputs, with auto-advance and validation (two different letters A–Z). 'Show letter grid' is offered but not wired. Select a word, then 'Set aside' or 'Place in pick'; 'Confirm pick' is a second step. Tap a shelf word to restore it.","motion":"Word size eases over 0.35s as the list shrinks.","accessibility":"Real labelled text inputs use the system keyboard. Words are buttons with aria-pressed. The two-step pick prevents accidental endings.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"The narrowing list feels physical and readable. But relying on the system keyboard is less tactile on phones, the 26-key alternative is only promised, and the flowing layout varies from board to board.","mockup":"shortlist-D.html","path":"team-01/shortlist-D.html"}]},{"id":"second-sense","name":"Second Sense","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The live game has two narrow columns of noun (blue) and verb (teal) clue cards, then a 'Your two senses' panel with an answer field. Long clues wrap to three lines in the narrow columns, which makes a tall stack on phones. The chosen noun and verb are not visually joined to the word you type. The director also raised an unconfirmed ordering hypothesis: some noun/verb pairs appear aligned by list position in No. 018. The sample in these mockups is consistent with that observation (HAWK, CLUB and MATCH sit at matching positions), and it remains unverified.","clarityRisk":"Low to medium for rules. The pairing step (choose one of each, then type) needs to read as one action.","review":{"slug":"second-sense","name":"Second Sense","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Two narrow columns of noun (blue) and verb (teal) clue cards, then a 'Your two senses' panel with an answer field and 'Choose clues ↑'. Long clues wrap to three lines in narrow columns, which makes a tall stack. Ordering hypothesis (not a confirmed bug): in No. 018, by the director's reading of the clues, noun 1 pairs with verb 1, noun 2 with verb 2 and noun 4 with verb 4, while the remaining two are swapped. Source check: app.mjs renders puzzle.nouns and puzzle.verbs in the order supplied, with no client-side shuffle; the files that set that order (puzzles.mjs, engine.mjs) are not in the supplied input, so whether verb order is independently shuffled can't be determined. If the two lists were independently and uniformly shuffled, three or more aligned pairs would happen on about 9% of boards (11/120), so a single board is weak evidence.","clarityRisk":"Low-medium for rules. Possible pairing leakage is a content/ordering question, not a visual one. To confirm or rule it out, inspect the board generator or count aligned positions across many boards: independent shuffling averages about one aligned pair per board.","bestDesignOpportunity":"When a noun and a verb are selected, the two cards slide together into one answer slot, and the typed word sits on the seam between them. Solved pairs lock into a stacked ledger showing both senses. Use full-width clue rows on phone instead of two narrow columns. Optional, separate: a three-sense bonus round.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Two-Faced Plate. The chosen noun and verb become the top and bottom faces of one plate with the word field between them, so 'one word, two meanings' is a single object. Borrow B's dictionary-entry ledger for solved words. Clue-order leakage is a content question, not a visual one; check the generator.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Two-Faced Plate","summary":"A central plate: the top face (blue wash, italic 'n.') shows the chosen noun meaning, the middle has a large underlined word field with First letter and Check, and the bottom face (teal wash, 'v.') shows the chosen verb meaning. Noun clues list on the left and verb clues on the right. Solved clues turn dashed with their word in green.","layout":"On desktop the plate sits in the middle (340px, sticky) with a clue column on each side. On phones the plate moves above both lists.","interaction":"Tap a noun and a verb, and each appears on its face of the plate. Choosing a verb scrolls the word field into view and focuses it. Check enables once both meanings and a word are present (message only). The first-letter hint is described, not wired.","motion":"Smooth scroll to the plate after the second choice. No other animation.","accessibility":"The word field is a labelled input with autocapitalise. Clues use aria-pressed. The n./v. badges duplicate the colour meaning. Enter does not check in this prototype, so revision 2 relabels the button 'Check' instead of 'Check ↵'.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: checks and hints used, five solved marks, no words.","tradeoff":"Joins the two senses into one object, which is the core idea of the game. But on phones the plate plus two full lists makes a long page, and the plate repeats clue text already in the list.","mockup":"second-sense-A.html","path":"team-01/second-sense-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Dictionary Page","summary":"The game is one white page. At the top, a draft entry reads like a dictionary line: a headword input, then 'n.' and the chosen noun definition, then 'v.' and the chosen verb definition. Below, nouns (n.1–n.5) and verbs (v.a–v.e) are ruled serif lists. Solved words are set below as finished entries, e.g. 'BAT n. Flying mammal… v. Strike a ball…'.","layout":"One article (max 980px): draft entry with Check / First letter & length / Clear, then two columns of definitions on desktop (stacked on phones), then the found entries.","interaction":"Choose definitions from the lists; they fill the draft line in place. Type the headword and press Enter or Check (message only). Clear resets the draft.","motion":"Smooth scroll to the headword after the second choice. Otherwise static.","accessibility":"Serif text at 17–20px. Selected rows use a coloured inset bar plus wash. Enter on the headword is wired. Definitions are full-width rows, which avoids three-line wrapping in narrow columns.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"This is the most adult and editorial, and the solved-entry ledger is a satisfying record. Its DM Serif page now sits closest to the live home page's type. But it is the least physical of the four, with no tactile pieces.","mockup":"second-sense-B.html","path":"team-01/second-sense-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Connector","summary":"Noun clues on the left and verb clues on the right each end in a socket. A warm-white hub with an ink rule sits in the middle (revision 2 replaced the old dark ink panel). It holds 'ONE WORD FOR BOTH', chips showing which n./v. are selected, a large word input, Letter hint and Check. On desktop, blue and teal SVG wires run from the selected clues into the hub.","layout":"On desktop, three columns with the hub at 250px. On phones the wires are hidden and the hub becomes a sticky bottom panel.","interaction":"Tap one clue on each side, and the wires redraw (and on resize). Type in the hub and Check (message only). The mockup opens with a partial draft 'HAW' to show a draft state.","motion":"Wires redraw on each selection. No easing.","accessibility":"The hub input has a label. Chips state 'n. 1' or 'v. 1' as text, and the wires are decorative (aria-hidden). Enter is not wired, so the button now reads 'Check' rather than 'Check ↵'.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"The wires make the pairing literal on desktop. But they vanish on phones, where the design falls back to two lists plus a sticky hub panel that takes vertical space.","mockup":"second-sense-C.html","path":"team-01/second-sense-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Word First","summary":"Reverses the order: type a hunch first in a large field, then attach a verb and a noun meaning to it from radio lists. Attached meanings appear as removable tags under the field. A four-step strip (Word · Verb · Noun · Check) tracks progress, and found words show as green chips.","layout":"Single column (max 780px): steps, field with Check, tags, then noun and verb radio groups, then found words and the hint.","interaction":"Type a word, choose one noun and one verb (radio), and detach either with ✕. Enter submits the form (message only). The mockup opens with 'WIND' typed and a verb attached.","motion":"The step strip updates as each part is supplied.","accessibility":"Uses a real form with Enter to submit, role=radiogroup lists with aria-checked, and labelled detach buttons.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"Fits players who think of the word first, and has the cleanest keyboard path. But it reverses the intended clue-first reading, and the hint (first letter for a chosen noun) needs the noun attached first, which the copy notes.","mockup":"second-sense-D.html","path":"team-01/second-sense-D.html"}]},{"id":"sideways","name":"Sideways","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"James found it too easy and pointed out that solving three columns forces the fourth, which is true given each row's fixed letters. The live layout is legible and orderly: four clue cards (A–D), five four-letter strips with ← → buttons and a live column readout. The arrow buttons double the number of targets, and the forced last column removes the final decision.","clarityRisk":"Low. The problem is depth, not comprehension.","review":{"slug":"sideways","name":"Sideways","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Four clue cards (A–D), five four-letter strips with ← → buttons, and a live column readout (CLOND, BROWM, GTAON, SRAID). Legible and orderly. The arrow buttons double the target count, and the fixed row multiset means the last column is always forced, which removes the final decision.","clarityRisk":"Low for rules; the problem is depth, not comprehension.","bestDesignOpportunity":"One direction: drag strips directly on a rail with detents (no arrow buttons), with column guides tinted only while a clue is selected. Optional mechanical experiment (separate): a decoy fifth column or a reversible strip so the last column isn't forced.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A — Drum Lock, the only direction for this replacement candidate. The real fix for 'too easy' is the separately labelled spare-letter-drum experiment, which needs new validated boards.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Drum Lock","summary":"Each row is a softly shaded paper drum of four letters with side turn keys. Selecting a clue (A–D) tints that column's reading window teal down all five drums. An untinted readout under the board shows the current columns (CLOND, BROWM, GTAON, SRAID at the start). Green appears only after a Check, and only for that arrangement.","layout":"On desktop the 480px machine (clue grid, column heads, drums, readout) sits beside a 300px panel (Undo, Nudge, Check board, stats, experiment box). On phones it stacks.","interaction":"Turn drums with the ← → keys (letters wrap). Tap a clue or column head to light its window. Check scores the current columns locally against the sample answers (GRAIN, STAND, CROWD, BLOOM), and the result clears on the next move. Undo does not refund moves. Nudge shows a message only.","motion":"Drum cells slide 18px in the turn direction, then spring back into place.","accessibility":"Turn keys are 44×56px and labelled 'Turn row 1 left'. Drums are groups labelled with their letters. Check results are shown with ✓ and text as well as colour.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: moves, checks and nudges, solved or not, no letters.","tradeoff":"Keeps the arrow keys the director suggested replacing with direct drag, and it does not change the forced fourth column. The spare-drum experiment would, but it needs new boards.","mockup":"sideways-A.html","path":"team-01/sideways-A.html"}]},{"id":"tradeoff","name":"Tradeoff","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"James likes Tradeoff a lot and also called it a bit too easy. Its raised letter tile is the best-received material on the site. Weaknesses: six identical bordered row cards make a long stack, a selected letter has no strong lifted state, and progress and goal text are 13–14px. Players can't easily see which rows are still off or what a swap will produce.","clarityRisk":"Low to medium. The swap rule is simple, but feedback on which swaps helped is thin.","review":{"slug":"tradeoff","name":"Tradeoff","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Six clue rows, each a bordered card with five raised letter tiles, a short rule line ('Each word starts one letter from its clue'), Undo and 'A little nudge'. The raised tile is the best-received material on the site. Weaknesses: six identical cards make a long stack; a selected or 'carried' letter has no visible lifted state in the capture; progress dots and goal text are 13–14px.","clarityRisk":"Low-medium. Players can't see which rows are still off, and there is no feedback on which swaps helped.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Treat it as the reference for the system's tile grammar. Remove the cards: rows sit on one recessed tray with hairline dividers. A selected tile lifts with a blue shadow while valid destinations glow, and a solved row seats its tiles with an end tick. Optional, separate: a 'tight' bonus round with a published swap par and one decoy letter, keeping the daily the same length.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"C — Blocks & Rails. Rows sit in recessed grooves, letters are thick raised blocks that lift when picked, solved rows seat flat with an end tick, and a par meter shows swaps against five. Borrow B's two-word swap preview. Keep D's unpinned-clue bonus as the separate, optional harder experiment that answers 'too easy'; it needs its own validated boards.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Trading Ledger","summary":"Card-free ruled rows: number, clue and five raised tiles per row. A ticker line above shows swaps, words home (n/6), nudges, undos and 'par 5 · perfect = 5, no nudge, no undo'. Solved rows show 'STUNT → STINT' in green under the clue.","layout":"Single column (max 860px). On desktop the tiles sit right of the clue; on phones, below it.","interaction":"Tap a letter to lift it (it rises 5px and turns blue), then tap any other unlocked letter, in any row, to trade. Solved rows lock. Undo restores the board, but the swap still counts. Ctrl/Cmd+Z and Esc are wired. Nudge shows a message only.","motion":"Lifted tile rises with a blue shadow. Both traded tiles pop in with a springy 0.42s animation.","accessibility":"Tiles are buttons labelled 'R, letter 3 of STRNT'. The ticker is aria-live. Solved tiles are disabled with green wash plus the ✓ row number.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: swaps vs par 5, nudges, undos, six solved marks, no letters.","tradeoff":"Clean, and keeps every clue next to its row. But it is close to the current structure, so it improves polish more than it changes the experience.","mockup":"tradeoff-A.html","path":"team-01/tradeoff-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Trade Tray","summary":"Only unsolved words stay on the board, and solved ones collapse to green chips at the top. Tiles are larger (24–36px letters). A sticky bottom tray shows the lifted letter as a big blue block, 'Lifted from STRNT', and a live preview of both resulting words for whichever partner you hover or focus.","layout":"Single column (max 640px): solved chips, word plates with clue below each, counts line, and the sticky trade tray with icon-only Undo and Nudge.","interaction":"Tap to lift, tap to trade. Hovering or focusing a partner previews both words (e.g. STUNT · HORSE), including the within-word case. Esc puts the letter back.","motion":"Lifted tile rises 6px. Traded tiles scale in with a springy 0.45s pop.","accessibility":"Focus triggers the same preview as hover, so keyboard users get it too. The tray is aria-live. The icon buttons have aria-labels.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"The two-word preview is the most useful feedback in the set and makes swaps deliberate. Hiding solved rows shortens the board, but players lose sight of the completed words they might reason from.","mockup":"tradeoff-B.html","path":"team-01/tradeoff-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Blocks & Rails","summary":"Each word sits in a recessed warm groove, and letters are raised warm-white blocks with a two-step edge (revision 2 replaced the old five-layer metallic stack). A lifted block turns blue and rises 6px, and every possible partner gets a dashed blue outline. Solved grooves seat their blocks flat in green with an end ✓. A segmented par meter fills one segment per swap and turns amber past five.","layout":"Single column (max 900px). On desktop the clue sits left and the groove right; on phones the clue is above the groove. The meter, count, Undo and Nudge sit beneath.","interaction":"With a mouse, drag a block onto another to trade (a tilted ghost follows the pointer). On touch, tap one block, then its partner. Undo keeps the swap count. Esc and Ctrl/Cmd+Z are wired.","motion":"Blocks rise on hover and lift with a deeper shadow when picked. The drag ghost tilts −4°. Seated rows drop flat.","accessibility":"Blocks are buttons with full labels. Partners are marked by an outline as well as colour. Drag is mouse-only; tap-tap is the touch and keyboard path.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A, using the par meter's swaps vs five.","tradeoff":"This is the strongest physical expression of the tile material James likes, and the par meter gives the light challenge he wants without a rule change. Amber for over-par is outside the handoff's state palette (gold is reserved for targets), so it needs a decision. The thick blocks also make each row taller.","mockup":"tradeoff-C.html","path":"team-01/tradeoff-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Clue Rail + Unpinned Bonus","summary":"Clues move into a separate rail beside a compact 6×5 tile grid. On phones the rail collapses to the clue for the row you are touching, with an 'All clues' toggle. A mode switch offers 'Bonus · unpinned clues', labelled as an experiment, in which clues are listed in no particular order and row numbers disappear, so you must also work out which clue each word is chasing.","layout":"On desktop the rail is on the left and the grid on the right. On phones the active clue sits above the grid. The bonus mode shows an explanatory note and a shuffled clue list, ticking clues as words are solved.","interaction":"Tapping a letter focuses its row's clue. Trading works as in A. Daily and Bonus keep separate local boards and counts.","motion":"Lifted tile rises 4px. Otherwise static.","accessibility":"Rail entries are buttons with focus outlines. The mode buttons use aria-pressed. The bonus note states that the Daily score and streak are unaffected.","sharing":"Not shown. The bonus would report separately from the Daily.","tradeoff":"The only concept that addresses 'a bit too easy' with a mechanic. It reuses today's words for illustration only, so real bonus boards would need validation. Separating clues from tiles weakens the at-a-glance clue-to-row link in the Daily.","mockup":"tradeoff-D.html","path":"team-01/tradeoff-D.html"}]},{"id":"inbetween","name":"Inbetween","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"James rated it 6/10 and kept it. He asked for drag-and-drop with strong placement visuals and for harder clues, and he approved the blue selected/drag highlight and a side explainer that mirrors it. In the live game the six pair chips (UN, RL, RE, AD, IT, AM) sit in a teal panel. Each base word has five '+' gap boxes in a strip beneath the letters rather than between them, and serif base letters next to sans pair chips weaken the 'this pair fits here' reading.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The insertion point is shown indirectly by boxes under the word.","review":{"slug":"inbetween","name":"Inbetween","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Six pair chips (UN, RL, RE, AD, IT, AM) in a teal panel above six clue rows. Each row shows a four-letter serif base word with five '+' gap boxes in a separate strip underneath. The approved blue drag state works, but the gaps live below the letters rather than between them, and serif base letters next to sans pair chips weaken the 'this pair fits here' reading.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The insertion point is shown indirectly (boxes under the letters).","bestDesignOpportunity":"Make gaps physical. Letters sit on a rail with real slots between them that open as a dragged pair approaches (letters slide apart and the blue preview sits inside the word). Keep the side explainer mirroring the blue drag state exactly as approved. Use one typeface for pairs and words. Harder clues are a separate content task.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Insertion Rail. Real gaps between letters widen and turn blue as soon as a pair is held, the in-word preview uses the approved blue, and the side guide mirrors the selected pair exactly as James asked. Use B's spreading-letter animation as its motion layer. Treat C's harder clues as a separate editorial and bonus experiment.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Insertion Rail","summary":"Each base word is a row of large letters with thin gap rails between and around them. Holding a pair widens every gap and washes it blue. A preview strip shows the built word with the pair highlighted in blue, plus a 'Place pair' button. A sticky side guide shows the selected blue PR pair → S[PR]ING and three numbered steps.","layout":"On desktop the board column (bank, six rows, stats, actions) sits beside a 300px sticky guide. On phones it is a single column, and the guide follows the board.","interaction":"Drag a pair onto a gap with pointer events (touch and mouse), or tap a pair then tap a gap. The preview is free; 'Place pair' confirms, and a wrong confirm costs one attempt and keeps the pair. 'Clear choice' and Nudge (message only) sit below.","motion":"Gaps widen over 0.2s when a pair is held. A tilted drag ghost follows the finger, and the gap under it turns solid blue.","accessibility":"Gap buttons speak 'Insert AM between L and E'. Each gap's hit area extends over half of each neighbouring letter. The guide gives the working path (Tab and Enter) and now says arrow-key movement between gaps is proposed but not wired.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: attempts and nudges, six solved marks, no pairs or words.","tradeoff":"This is the most direct answer to James's feedback, keeping the approved blue preview and mirrored guide. The two-step preview-then-place adds a tap compared with drop-to-commit, but it protects the attempt count.","mockup":"inbetween-A.html","path":"team-01/inbetween-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Split & Spread","summary":"Letters are raised tiles separated by thin seams. When a held pair passes a seam, the word physically splits and the two blue pair letters grow into the gap, so the preview is the word itself. Solved words keep the inserted pair in solid green. A sticky dock holds the six pairs and an inline example: SING + PR → S PR ING.","layout":"Single column (max 720px): six rows, a live message, then the sticky bottom dock with pairs, stats, Nudge and Clear.","interaction":"Drag a pair across words and the seam under the finger previews live. Tap a seam to preview. 'Place pair' or 'Close up' each preview. A wrong confirm shakes the inserted letters red and costs one attempt.","motion":"Inserted letters grow from zero width with a spring (0.38s, staggered). Solved words snap from wide letter-spacing to normal. Wrong fits shake.","accessibility":"Seams speak 'Preview RL between U and E'. Hit areas extend 10px each side. The dock example has an aria-label.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"This is the most satisfying placement visual. But seams are thin targets that rely on the extended hit area, and the explainer is a compact inline example rather than the full side panel James approved.","mockup":"inbetween-B.html","path":"team-01/inbetween-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Clue Focus + Harder Clues","summary":"One word at a time on a large card. A six-word overview strip sits on top. The clue is set at 24–36px serif, with a 'Standard clue / Harder clue BONUS' toggle above it. The base word is shown with dashed '+' gaps at up to 54px. The result line shows the built word with the pair highlighted.","layout":"Single column (max 760px): overview strip, focus card (toggle, clue, word, result, pairs grid, ← Word / Place pair / Word →).","interaction":"Choose a pair, tap a '+' gap to preview it in place, then Place pair. Navigate between words with the strip or the arrows. Toggling to Standard is noted as recorded in the bonus result.","motion":"The chosen gap expands to show the pair. Otherwise static.","accessibility":"This option has the largest clue and word type. The toggle uses aria-pressed. Gaps are labelled 'Gap n'.","sharing":"Not shown. The bonus would be scored separately from the Daily.","tradeoff":"Answers 'harder clues' directly, but the harder clues are editorial samples, not validated content. Focus mode hides the other five words, and it has no drag-and-drop, which James asked for.","mockup":"inbetween-C.html","path":"team-01/inbetween-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Dominoes & Slabs","summary":"Pairs are white dominoes with a centre divider and pip, in a recessed tray. Base words are engraved letters on slab plates with recessed notches between letters. When a domino is held, the notches widen; on preview, a notch opens to a blue block showing the pair. 'Set BARREN' confirms, and solved slabs turn green.","layout":"On desktop a 200px sticky domino tray sits beside a two-column grid of slabs. On phones the tray sits above one column of slabs.","interaction":"Drag a domino into a notch (pointer events), or tap one and then a notch. 'Lift out' cancels a preview. A wrong 'Set' costs one attempt.","motion":"Notches widen when armed and open to full pair width on hover or preview. The held domino lifts 4px, and a tilted domino ghost follows the drag.","accessibility":"Notches speak 'Insert RE at notch 4 of BARN'. Hit areas extend 10px. Revision 2 reduced the engraving: slab letters are full charcoal ink on a recessed paper line.","sharing":"Not shown. Same as A.","tradeoff":"This is the most tactile material, and the domino reads clearly as an unbreakable pair. But each slab is a card, so six cards plus a domino tray make the page busier than A's single rail.","mockup":"inbetween-D.html","path":"team-01/inbetween-D.html"}]},{"id":"kimi-cairn","name":"Cairn","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The game is a clean 3→7 letter anagram climb, but the current board doesn't show that climb. A tiny five-pill progress bar and a decorative stone icon sit apart from a generic card with dashed slots. Beige secondary buttons drift toward the brown palette James rejected. The Clean/Assisted/Shown legend floats at the bottom with no link to any level. The 'perfect summit' result image was called out as looking dumb.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The rack-to-slot input is clear, but 'a new letter drops in and you must reuse everything' is only explained in the help dialog, and the reason for the five levels isn't visible.","review":{"slug":"kimi-cairn","name":"Cairn","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Corrected capture, No. 628 at level 1. One card holds: five level pills plus a faint stacked-slab glyph; 'Level 1 · 3 letters' with a two-line instruction and '0 of 3 placed'; a pale blue drop well with three dashed slots; a rack of three raised letter tiles (T H A); a shuffle / blue ENTER / backspace row; 'Hint · 1 left' and 'Skip level'; and a Clean/Assisted/Shown legend. It is legible and calm, and the raised rack tiles already fit the home's subtle tactile tiles (warm-white face, fine line, short flat edge). Weaknesses: the five pills are near-identical grey lozenges with no letter counts; the slab glyph is decoration and doesn't yet reflect solved levels; hint, skip, shuffle and backspace are beige; about 180px of empty space sits inside the outlined game frame (see cross-game findings). The cartoon summit image James criticised is an end state and is not in this start-of-game capture.","clarityRisk":"Medium. From level 2 on, the newly dropped letter must look different from the carried letters. Level 1 has no carried letters, so that state isn't visible in the capture.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Restrained stacked slabs. The faint slab glyph in the current card is the right seed: make it the real progress display. Each solved word becomes a horizontal stone slab with engraved letters, stacking from three to seven letters, with each level's new letter marked by a blue inset. This replaces the five grey pills. Replace the summit picture with a typographic result of five slabs marked clean, assisted or shown. Never a cartoon mountain.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Stairstep. The board becomes the climb, and each solved word stays engraved on its stair with its status mark. Pair it with D's result card, which replaces the summit picture.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Stairstep","summary":"Five left-aligned slabs grow one slot wider per level, from 3 at the bottom to 7 at the top, so the board itself is the climb. The active stair is enlarged and takes the letters directly.","layout":"On desktop the climb panel sits beside a slim side column (how levels are marked, what happens next, keyboard). On phones it is a single column: stairs, then the rack, then Enter/Shuffle/Delete, then quiet Hint and Skip.","interaction":"Tap a rack tile to drop it into the next empty slot on the active stair, and tap a placed letter to send it back. Typing and Enter are proposed for the live game but not wired in this prototype. A local preview button shows the two-letters-placed state.","motion":"Letters drop into slots with a short fall. On a solve, the word would set into the slab and the next stair would light (described, not prototyped).","accessibility":"Slots are buttons with spoken state ('Placed T, tap to remove'). Rack tiles are 64×68px. Status uses fill pattern as well as colour (solid, split, dashed). Live text reports '2 of 3 placed'.","sharing":"Use D's five-bar result card, coloured by status, with no words.","tradeoff":"The board is taller than today's on phones, though the stairs are compact (26–36px cells). Seven-letter stairs sit at the top, so the start of play is at the bottom of the panel.","mockup":"cairn-A.html","path":"team-02/cairn-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Monolith","summary":"One warm-white slab card holds only the current level, in very large type with round pebble keys. Progress is a five-stone side profile at top right, and secondary actions live in a sheet.","layout":"A single centred column (max 600px). A big '1/5' level number sits top-left with the stone profile top-right. Below are three 64–84px answer cells, three pebble keys and the Shuffle/Enter/Delete row, with a 'More' sheet for Hint, Skip and Rules.","interaction":"Tap pebbles to fill cells. 'More' expands a sheet of 56px rows (shown open via the local toggle).","motion":"Pebbles lift on hover and press down on tap. A solved slab would slide down and a new, wider slab rise in.","accessibility":"This is the largest type of the four options. The profile has an aria-label explaining that stones widen per level. The sheet uses aria-expanded.","sharing":"Same status-bar card as D, without the summit picture.","tradeoff":"Earlier words are not visible on the board, so players lose the sense of the tower. Hint and Skip are one tap further away.","mockup":"cairn-B.html","path":"team-02/cairn-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Carry and add","summary":"States the mechanic as an equation: a shallow tray of letter sorts → one rail of answer slots. A 3 +1 4 +1 5 +1 6 +1 7 chain makes the growth explicit.","layout":"On desktop the tray, an arrow and the rail sit side by side; on phones they stack vertically. The chain runs on top, controls are centred, and an expandable worked example uses the rules' own TEA + R → TEAR/RATE case.","interaction":"Tap sorts to fill the rail. 'How level 2 opens' expands the example, labelled as not today's letters.","motion":"In the example, the new letter R falls into place with a spring. In play, the dropped-in letter would do the same.","accessibility":"The chain has a spoken summary. Tall 56×76px sorts. The example is plain text.","sharing":"Status-bar card.","tradeoff":"The most instructional option, but the least atmospheric. The tray and rail duplicate letters on screen.","mockup":"cairn-C.html","path":"team-02/cairn-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Altitude rail and result card","summary":"A persistent altitude rail of five nodes (3→7 letters) frees the panel for input. Keycaps have a short, flat press. Includes a redesigned result card with five bars sized 3→7, coloured by Clean/Assisted/Shown.","layout":"Desktop has the rail on the left and the input panel on the right. On phones the rail turns horizontal above the panel. The result card preview opens below.","interaction":"Tap keycaps (typing is proposed but not wired in the prototype). 'Preview result card' toggles an illustrative card.","motion":"Keycaps depress 4px on press. The rail marker would slide up a node on each solve.","accessibility":"The rail is a nav with text labels. 68px keycaps. Card bars carry an aria-label listing each level's status.","sharing":"Card shows 'n of 5 levels solved', counts of clean and assisted, and five status bars, never the words. The statuses shown are illustrative.","tradeoff":"Separates progress from the board, so it is less integrated than A. The rail costs width on desktop.","mockup":"cairn-D.html","path":"team-02/cairn-D.html"}]},{"id":"kimi-morph","name":"Morph","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The core is readable, but the screen stacks GOAL card, START row, NEXT row, a counter, a big Check button, two beige buttons, two disabled buttons and a footnote, so the ladder itself never looks like a ladder. The NEXT row uses the same tile style as START, so it's unclear what is editable. Tapping a letter to swap it gives no visible picker until tapped.","clarityRisk":"Low to medium. The rules are simple, but it is unclear which row is editable, and the 'changed two letters' error only appears after submitting.","review":{"slug":"kimi-morph","name":"Morph","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Corrected capture, No. 64. 'Moves 0 · suggested 5', GOAL 'MESS' in a pale blue panel, a START 'BANG' row of light tiles, and an editable NEXT row of large solid-blue tiles. Below: '0 letters changed · change exactly one', a strong CHECK WORD, beige 'Show a route · 1 hint' / 'Give up', and disabled 'Undo rung' / 'Restart ladder'. The beige buttons don't match the home control vocabulary. About 120px of empty space sits inside the outlined game frame below the card; it is shared by all six Kimi pages and its cause is unverified (see cross-game findings). No rungs have been accepted yet, so how ladder history displays isn't visible.","clarityRisk":"Low. This is the clarity benchmark; the risk is losing it in a redesign.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Keep the tile input exactly. Add a ladder: accepted rungs stack between START and GOAL, with each rung's changed letter marked and the 'suggested 5' shown as five rung positions. Remove the reserved empty space under the card. Restyle the hint and give-up buttons as home secondary controls (warm-white, 1px line, ink text).","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A — Ladder rails, with D's live 'exactly one change' check added under the active rung. This keeps the strong readability James asked to preserve and makes input explicit.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Ladder rails","summary":"A literal ladder: the goal MESS is fixed in ink at the top and the start BANG at the bottom. Played rungs carry a green tick under the changed letter. Dashed rungs show the suggested five moves.","layout":"On desktop, the ladder panel (≤460px) sits beside a rules column with feedback and the hint/give-up actions. On phones it is a single column with the swap picker directly beneath the ladder.","interaction":"Tap a letter on the active rung to open an A–Z picker (auto-fill grid with 44px keys) for that column. Typing a four-letter word is the proposed keyboard alternative (not wired in the prototype). The picker is live.","motion":"On acceptance, the new rung would slide up into place and the dashed par rung above it would fill.","accessibility":"The active rung's letters are toggle buttons with aria-pressed. The picker states which letter is being swapped. Changed-letter ticks are positional, not colour-only.","sharing":"Rungs used vs suggested, plus Assisted if the route was shown, with no words.","tradeoff":"Tall on phones if a ladder runs long (each rung is 66px). The illustrative 'BAND' rung must not be read as a supplied route.","mockup":"morph-A.html","path":"team-02/morph-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Combination dials","summary":"Four letter dials in a warm, flat housing under a plain warm-white GOAL card. Turning one dial locks the other three, so the 'exactly one letter' rule is physically enforced before submitting.","layout":"Centred column: goal card with moves and suggested count, dial housing, Check plus Reset, a ladder trail of chips, then hint and give-up.","interaction":"▲/▼ buttons (44px) per dial are live in the prototype. Arrow keys on a focused dial and typing a word are proposed but not wired.","motion":"Letters roll vertically on turn. The moved dial gains a green frame while the others dim and show 'locked'.","accessibility":"Each window has an aria-label giving the letter. Lock state is shown as text, not only opacity. The read-out line is aria-live.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Reaching a distant letter takes many taps (B→M is 11 steps), so typing remains essential. More playful, and possibly slower for expert players.","mockup":"morph-B.html","path":"team-02/morph-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Bridge and target columns","summary":"START and GOAL face each other across a dotted bridge of five nodes. In the editor, the goal letter is printed under every column, and a column turns green once it matches.","layout":"Bridge across the top, then a centred editor with four 76px column buttons over goal letters. A QWERTY key bank sits below (48px tall keys).","interaction":"Select a column (outlined in ink, so green only means 'matches the goal'), then tap its new letter; physical typing is proposed but not wired. Live text warns when two letters have been changed and says it costs nothing.","motion":"The bridge node advances one step per accepted rung.","accessibility":"Columns carry aria-pressed. Matches use a rule plus colour. The legend states what the top and bottom rows mean.","sharing":"Bridge glyph showing moves vs suggested.","tradeoff":"On a 320px phone the keys are narrower than 44px, as on a system keyboard. Showing goal letters per column slightly eases the puzzle.","mockup":"morph-C.html","path":"team-02/morph-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Typed editor with live checks","summary":"Keyboard-first: one giant text field for the next word, with three live checks underneath (four letters, exactly one change with the diff named, not a repeat). Word-list membership is honestly stated as 'checked on Enter'.","layout":"On desktop, the focus column sits beside a ladder log. On phones it is a single column using the native keyboard.","interaction":"Type freely. Demo buttons fill BEND (two changes) and BAND (one change) to show the check states.","motion":"Each check animates from neutral to ✓ or ✕ as you type.","accessibility":"A real input with a label. Checks are an aria-live list using icons and text. There is no colour-only meaning.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Least visual, and the ladder is less present. It depends on the system keyboard on phones, which is fine for Morph but less tactile.","mockup":"morph-D.html","path":"team-02/morph-D.html"}]},{"id":"kimi-tangle","name":"Tangle · grid","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"This is the grid word-trail game, distinct from the interleaved-bead Tangle. The board itself is solid, but the screen above it is busy: a 4×4/5×5 toggle, points, a medal label, a word count, a slider-like track and an instructions box. The traced path has no strong visual, so drags are hard to read. 'Found' is a bare list. James asked about 5×5 and repeatedly hit vocabulary gaps, so where accepted words appear and how the 5×5 board is presented both matter.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Adjacency and path order are hard to see mid-drag, and the purpose of the '5×5 Preview' is unclear.","review":{"slug":"kimi-tangle","name":"Tangle (word trails)","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Corrected capture, No. 124. At the top of the card, a '4×4 Daily | 5×5 Preview' segmented switch. Then a status line ('0 points · No medal yet · 0/84 words'), '18 more points to Gold · 97 available', and a three-stop Bronze 6 / Silver 12 / Gold 18 track. Then a boxed instruction ('Drag a word and release on a letter to check. Or tap letters, then Check.'), a 4×4 grid of large raised tiles in a pale tray, a beige 'Clue · 1 available' with '0 misses', and 'Found (0)'. The board is legible and the tiles are generous. The problem is the numbers: points, words-found-of-84, points-to-Gold, points-available and three thresholds all come before the board. '0/84 words' implies a completion target, but the goal is Gold at 18 points. The 5×5 Preview segment has the same weight as the daily. Diagonal adjacency is stated only in the help. James's feedback was about vocabulary, not visuals.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Goal maths and diagonal adjacency need on-board confirmation, and the words/points split invites the wrong target. The name collides with the other Tangle (interleaved words).","bestDesignOpportunity":"A physical trace: a thick stroke snaps between tile centres with a live word readout above the grid, and accepted words fly their points into the three-stage track, which is the one progress display. '84 words' and '97 available' move to stats. In the hub, label it 'Tangle · Word trails' to tell it apart. 5×5 already exists as a Preview: demote it from an equal segment to a secondary 'Larger board' link below the board, not the daily default.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Engraved plate, using B's split workspace above 820px and C's 'optional bonus after Gold' placement for the 5×5 board, if James wants it.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Engraved plate","summary":"Letters sit in shallow wells on one warm-white plate. The trace is a thick translucent green ribbon, so path order can't be misread. The medal goals become a precise tick ruler: Bronze 6, Silver 12, Gold 18.","layout":"Single column (max 540px): board-size switch, a points/words line, the ruler, the current word with its point value, then the plate, help line, Clear/Check/Clue and the Found summary.","interaction":"Tap adjacent letters, diagonals included; dragging is proposed but only tapping is wired in the prototype. Tapping the last letter again removes it, and a demo traces HERB.","motion":"The ribbon draws between well centres as you move. On acceptance it would fade into the Found list.","accessibility":"Every cell is a button labelled with letter, row and column. The current word is aria-live. The ruler has an aria-label stating all three goals.","sharing":"Points, medal and word count (n/84), with no words.","tradeoff":"The ribbon can cover letters on very small screens; it is kept at 55% opacity to stay readable.","mockup":"tangle-A.html","path":"team-02/tangle-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Split workspace","summary":"A desk layout: a flat, high-contrast board with numbered path order on the left, and a medal ladder plus found words grouped by length on the right. On phones a two-tab switch keeps the board full width.","layout":"Two columns above 820px. Below that, Board and Found tabs, with the word tray above the board.","interaction":"Tap to trace (drag proposed, not wired); tiles show 1, 2, 3… path order. The tabs switch locally. Found words are an illustrative mid-game state (THE, HUT, PIE, BREW) traced from today's grid.","motion":"A newly accepted word would drop into its length row.","accessibility":"Strong 2px ink borders and solid fills for selection. The tabs use role=tab with aria-selected.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Flatter and less physical. The illustrative found words were not checked against the game's word list.","mockup":"tangle-B.html","path":"team-02/tangle-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Keycaps and bonus board","summary":"Warm-white keycaps with a short, flat press as you trace, joined by a thin green wire. The medal track ends in an optional 'Bonus 5×5' step that opens after Gold, with its own score.","layout":"Single column: a four-step goal track, a points line, a recessed keycap deck, the current word, actions, and a collapsible bonus card (no 5×5 letters shown).","interaction":"Trace keys locally. Expand the bonus card to read how the optional round would work.","motion":"Keys travel down 4px when selected. The wire draws with end dots.","accessibility":"Key labels match the other options. The bonus card uses details/summary.","sharing":"Daily card unchanged; the bonus would be reported separately.","tradeoff":"Making 5×5 an earned bonus is a product decision (it changes where the 5×5 mode lives), not a rule change. Keycaps take more vertical space.","mockup":"tangle-C.html","path":"team-02/tangle-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Type to trace","summary":"Letters float on a fine dot lattice without tile boxes. Typing a word lights every legal trail on the board (solid for the first, dashed for alternates), and impossible spellings are explained before submitting.","layout":"On desktop the field is on the left and the typing panel with score cells on the right. On phones they stack.","interaction":"Type or tap. Demos: HER (two trails, via either H), HOT, and TOW (no trail). The path finding is live; the Enter and Esc shortcuts are labelled but not wired.","motion":"Trails redraw on every keystroke.","accessibility":"This option gives keyboard users the most direct play. The status line is aria-live and states the trail count.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"This is an optional input-method change, separate from the core UI. It makes typed play much easier than dragging, and the lightweight letters are less tactile.","mockup":"tangle-D.html","path":"team-02/tangle-D.html"}]},{"id":"kimi-hunch","name":"Hunch","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The grouped black/white dots are the right mechanic, but the screen still looks like a five-column, six-row tile grid with a QWERTY keyboard under it, which is the exact silhouette James compared to Wordle. The dots are small and live in a separate legend line. The 'not aligned to letters' rule is stated rather than designed.","clarityRisk":"Medium. New players will still expect per-letter colours, and duplicate-letter counting needs an in-context explanation.","review":{"slug":"kimi-hunch","name":"Hunch","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Corrected capture, No. 150 before the first guess. 'Guess 1 of 6' with a beige 'Reveal a letter', then a 6×5 grid of empty square tiles in a pale tray. The grid is left-aligned with an empty right-hand column, presumably the dot area (it is empty before any guess, so dot rendering isn't visible). Below: the legend '● Black: exact · ○ White: elsewhere · Dots are grouped, not aligned to letters', and a QWERTY keyboard with letter keys about 30px wide, a dark-teal Enter and a beige backspace. The six-by-five grid of squares is still the strongest Wordle signal, even with grouped dots.","clarityRisk":"Medium-high. Grouped feedback is less specific, so the peg area must be unmistakable and must never read as per-letter colour. Keyboard keys are narrow for phone use.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Break the silhouette, not the rules. Each guess is a single engraved code bar of five letters, with a separate peg block to its right where black and white pegs seat in holes (Mastermind-like), using the column the current layout already reserves. No green or yellow letter feedback (the home green stays in chrome and primary actions only). Keep keyboard input. Ten keys across 393px can't reach 44px width, so use the full card width and at least 52px key height, the usual phone-keyboard trade-off.","priority":"high"},"recommended":"A — Peg board. It is the most clearly differentiated, and the physical key-peg well makes 'grouped, not aligned' obvious. Borrow B's numerals in labels for screen readers.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Peg board","summary":"A physical deduction board: neutral round letter pegs, and on each row a recessed well of five key-peg holes filled black first, then white. There is no per-letter colour anywhere.","layout":"Single column (max 520px): guess counter with Reveal a letter, the board (six rows), the dot key, a 'why aren't pegs under letters?' expander, and a 52px-tall keyboard.","interaction":"Type or tap keys in the live game; in the prototype the keyboard is visual only. The expander is a working local toggle with a plain explanation.","motion":"After Enter, key pegs would drop into the well one by one: blacks, then whites.","accessibility":"Each well has an aria-label such as '3 exact, 1 elsewhere'. The empty row reads 'not yet scored'. Black and white pegs differ in fill and outline, not only colour.","sharing":"Rows of grouped pegs only, which can't spoil the word or resemble a colour grid.","tradeoff":"Letter pegs shrink to about 30px on a 320px phone, which is readable but small. The illustrative practice state hides its answer.","mockup":"hunch-A.html","path":"team-02/hunch-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Ledger","summary":"A typographic table with columns #, Guess, Exact and Elsewhere. Numerals carry precision and the dots are for scanning. The side panel explains duplicates using the rules' ALLEY/APPLE example.","layout":"Desktop: table and keyboard on the left, rules panel on the right. Phone: single column; the second column keeps its numeral and drops repeated dots.","interaction":"Type, with the caret shown in the guess cell, or use the on-screen keyboard. Neither is wired in the prototype; the ledger is a static illustrative state.","motion":"Counts would tick up from zero on reveal.","accessibility":"A real table with header cells. Numerals mean nothing depends on telling dots apart.","sharing":"Numerals per guess, e.g. '1·2 / 3·1'.","tradeoff":"Feels analytical rather than playful, and the guess grid disappears into a table.","mockup":"hunch-B.html","path":"team-02/hunch-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Deduction notebook","summary":"Guess cards with dot pills, plus a keyboard with a Type/Note switch. In Note mode the player marks letters 'ruled out' or 'likely in'. These are their own notes, clearly labelled as never coming from the game.","layout":"Desktop: guesses on the left, notebook keyboard on the right. Phone: stacked.","interaction":"Note mode cycles a key through none → out → in, live in the prototype. Struck letters would mirror into the guess cards (shown statically here). Entering letters in Type mode is not wired.","motion":"A strike-through draws across the key.","accessibility":"Key labels speak the note state. The mode is a pressed toggle.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Adds a helper layer. Some players may feel it nudges toward Wordle-like keyboards, though nothing is auto-marked.","mockup":"hunch-C.html","path":"team-02/hunch-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Focus line","summary":"Hierarchy flipped: the guess being written is the biggest object on screen. Earlier guesses compress into a newest-first list with dots. A large dock keyboard sits below, and there is an illustrative result-card preview.","layout":"Single column: six-segment try meter, hero entry, 'dots appear here' slot, history list, keyboard, then the card toggle.","interaction":"Type or tap in the live game; the prototype keyboard is visual only. The card preview is a working local toggle.","motion":"On Enter, the hero row would shrink into the history list and its dots would appear.","accessibility":"54px keys. History rows include spoken dot counts.","sharing":"Card with grouped dots per guess (rows 4–5 invented and labelled).","tradeoff":"Past guesses are smaller, so cross-referencing earlier rows takes more effort.","mockup":"hunch-D.html","path":"team-02/hunch-D.html"}]},{"id":"kimi-circuit","name":"Circuit","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The cardinal quadrants and distinct blue/green/gold/red arcs are now correct and must stay. The large empty navy disc wastes the centre and is the darkest thing on a light page. Membership is repeated in four outlined cards. The same-arc restriction is only explained in text, and the 12-dash progress strip is unlabelled.","clarityRisk":"Medium to high. James asked how to tell the arcs apart, and the 'can't follow from the same arc' rule isn't visible at the moment of choosing.","review":{"slug":"kimi-circuit","name":"Circuit","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Corrected capture, No. 172. 'Words 0 · target 3 · Letters lit 0/12', a one-line instruction and a dashed entry track. Then a ring split on the cardinal axes into four quadrants: Arc 1 blue top-right (K B C), Arc 2 green bottom-right (U S H), Arc 3 gold bottom-left (A I R) and Arc 4 red top-left (W M E). Each letter is a cream disc with its arc number beneath, which is a good non-colour cue. (Correction: the earlier '1·CPS, 2·AOE' cards came from the help example, not the board.) The ring's centre is a large solid dark-navy disc that carries no state at the start. Four arc cards repeat the ring's contents below it. Then come seven controls: clear, backspace, teal 'Play word', disabled 'Undo word' / 'Restart chain', 'Route clue · 1 free', and a dashed beige 'Reveal route'. The geometry and colours were hard-won and accepted.","clarityRisk":"Medium. 'Consecutive letters from different arcs' and 'start with the last letter' need live feedback while typing. The duplicate arc cards and the long control stack push the board's purpose down the page.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Preserve the quadrants, cardinal splits, arc colours and arc numbers exactly. Turn the dead dark centre into a light recessed well that shows the current word and the chain drawn as chords across the ring. This also removes a dark panel from the light frame. During entry, the last letter's arc dims (illegal next) while the others stay bright. Accepted words list beneath as a linked chain with shared letters. Drop the duplicate arc cards on phone, and group clue and reveal into one quiet row.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Instrument dial. The current word moves into the dial face, letters on the forbidden arc dim with a stated reason, and a coverage strip by arc replaces the dashes. Offer C's quadrant keypad as the phone layout below about 360px if testing shows ring taps are tight.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Instrument dial","summary":"A flat printed dial with four bands in the approved blue/green/gold/red, split exactly on the 0°/90°/180°/270° axes with thin even dividers, and letter caps with a small flat edge showing arc numbers. A warm-white face shows the current word and 'Next: not Arc n'.","layout":"Desktop: dial on the left; on the right the next-word heading, rule, chain, a coverage strip by arc, and actions. Phone: stacked.","interaction":"Tap letters, or Tab to a cap and press Enter; typing letters is proposed but not wired. Same-arc taps are refused with a sentence explaining why. Live in the prototype from an illustrative HAWK-played state.","motion":"Lit caps invert to ink. The face word updates per letter.","accessibility":"Each cap is a role=button with an aria-label of letter, arc and allowed state, and a 62px hit area in a 400-unit viewBox (about 46px at 296px wide). Arc numbers are printed on every cap.","sharing":"Words used vs target, 12/12 lit, clue used or not.","tradeoff":"The ring needs at least 296px. Hit areas sit right at the 44px limit on the smallest phones.","mockup":"circuit-A.html","path":"team-02/circuit-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"String chords","summary":"The chain is drawn as numbered chords across the ring. A legal word always zig-zags between quadrants, so the rule becomes visible geometry. The blocked quadrant gets a faint wash.","layout":"Centred column: a large current word, a rule sentence, the ring card, an aria-live refusal line, a 4-up arc legend with compass labels, then actions.","interaction":"Tap letters to extend the dashed ink chord path. Played words stay as solid muted chords, so no chord colour competes with the arc colours.","motion":"Chords draw from letter to letter.","accessibility":"Letter buttons are labelled. The legend restates membership as text (Arc 1 · NE · KBC).","sharing":"A chord diagram with no letters would be a striking share image, if James wants one.","tradeoff":"Long chains can make the centre busy. Chord numbers help, but late in a round it gets dense.","mockup":"circuit-B.html","path":"team-02/circuit-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Quadrant keypad","summary":"For phones, the ring becomes a compact map and input moves to a 2×2 keypad in the same compass positions (Arc 4 NW, Arc 1 NE, Arc 3 SW, Arc 2 SE). The quadrant you just used greys out and says 'Not right after K'.","layout":"Desktop: map on the left, word and keypad on the right. Phone: a 220px map above the word, keypad and actions.","interaction":"Keys are 56px tall and about 40px wide at 320px, rising to 52px wide at 393px. Live in the prototype.","motion":"Blinking caret. The word letters are underlined in arc colours.","accessibility":"This option has the largest tap targets. Disabled keys carry aria-disabled and an overlay reason. Gold keys use ink letters for contrast.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Separates seeing the ring from touching it, so the circuit feels less tactile.","mockup":"circuit-C.html","path":"team-02/circuit-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Chain and coverage","summary":"The chain is the scoreboard: words as joined ink plates, with the next one opening on the shared letter. A 4×3 matrix grouped by arc shows exactly which letters still need lighting. Includes an illustrative result card.","layout":"Chain bar across the top. Desktop: ring on the left, coverage, actions and card on the right. Phone: stacked.","interaction":"Tap the ring. The matrix and chain update. Card preview toggle.","motion":"Matrix cells fill with their arc colour when lit.","accessibility":"The matrix is text plus colour. The ring centre states the next-arc rule.","sharing":"Twelve arc-coloured cells plus 'n words · target 3', never words. The finish shown is invented and labelled.","tradeoff":"More components on screen. The ring is slightly smaller on desktop.","mockup":"circuit-D.html","path":"team-02/circuit-D.html"}]},{"id":"kimi-quartet","name":"Quartet","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"James found it fun and asked that it not be an exact copy of another game. The live game shows sixteen words in a grid with Shuffle, Clear, Submit, a mistakes counter and 'Pair glow · 1 free'. Its help text adds 'One away…' and numbered difficulty tiers, and today's groups (chess pieces, hats, poem forms, rocks) plus the select-four, four-mistake structure read very close to a well-known publisher's grouping game. At 393px the Shuffle and Clear labels look crowded (to be verified live).","clarityRisk":"Low for rules. High for originality.","review":{"slug":"kimi-quartet","name":"Quartet","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Corrected capture, No. 184. 'Groups 0/4 · Mistakes ●●●●', an instruction with a live '0 of 4 selected', sixteen uppercase words in a 2×8 grid of wide raised tiles, Shuffle / Clear / 'Submit 0/4', and 'Pair glow · 1 free'. At 393px the Shuffle and Clear labels are crowded against their button edges, and 'Clear' appears clipped. Verify that live. The date reads 'Friday, Nov 6', where the other five Kimi pages use ISO dates. The help text adds 'One away…' and numbered difficulty tiers 1–4. Together with today's groups (chess pieces, hats, poem forms, rocks) and the select-four, four-mistake structure, these make it read even closer to a well-known publisher's grouping game, which is exactly the originality risk James raised.","clarityRisk":"Low for rules; high for originality.","bestDesignOpportunity":"One original direction: four empty, unlabelled lanes (trays) that words are dragged into, with a lane's theme label appearing only once it is full. Optional mechanical experiment (separate): a name-the-thread bonus, or one word that legitimately fits two groups and must be resolved by elimination.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Assembly Tray, as an original interaction layer. Before adoption, drop or reword the 'three of these four connect' near-miss message; it echoes the 'one away' convention the director flagged.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Assembly Tray","summary":"Instead of highlighting tiles in place, words drop into a recessed four-socket tray above the grid, and 'Lock quartet' commits it. Solved quartets become full-width bands, each with a group name. Revision 2 replaced the old navy and teal slabs with light blue, teal, ochre and clay washes, each with a coloured rule and dark text. A four-segment miss meter, 'Pair glow · 1 free' and Shuffle / Empty tray sit nearby.","layout":"Single column (max 760px): quartet count and miss meter, solved bands, the tray with Lock, a near-miss note, a 2-column word grid (4-column on wider screens), then controls.","interaction":"Tap a word to drop it into the next socket, and tap a socket to send it back. Lock checks the sample groups locally. A repeated wrong set costs no miss. Pair glow outlines two words from one quartet once. Shuffle reorders.","motion":"Words drop into sockets with a short fall and fade (0.25s). Solved bands drop in the same way.","accessibility":"Sockets speak 'Socket 2: ROOK'. Words use aria-pressed, and the near-miss note is role=status. The miss meter has text ('3 misses left'). Group names are the designer's reading of the words, as the review banner states.","sharing":"Not shown. The footer proposes misses and the order of quartets, never the words.","tradeoff":"The tray makes building a quartet a physical act distinct from tile-highlighting grids. But the underlying structure (16 words, four groups, four misses) is unchanged, so originality still depends on content and mechanic decisions beyond this UI.","mockup":"kimi-quartet-A.html","path":"team-01/kimi-quartet-A.html"}]},{"id":"deepseek-twin-hive","name":"Twin Hive","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The stretched-hex issue was patched, but the eight letters still sit in a 4×2 rack inside a rounded box inside a card, so the two gold letters read as just two more tiles. Found words become loose pills under a 'little space' heading, and the six-word goal is only a thin meter. 'Still needs A + M' is small helper text.","clarityRisk":"Low to medium. The rule is simple, but it should be built into the letter layout rather than only written below it.","review":{"slug":"deepseek-twin-hive","name":"Twin Hive","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Eight hex letters in two rows (gold A and M marked with small stars, six white), an entry field, Delete/Clear/Add word, a 0/6 progress bar, points and 'A little space for your first word'. It is compact now but plain: two rows of hexes aren't a hive, and the found-word area is empty filler.","clarityRisk":"Low-medium. The live 'Still needs A + M' text is good; repeats allowed and the four-letter minimum need equal visibility.","bestDesignOpportunity":"A true compact cluster: the two gold anchors side by side at the centre with six letters around them, each anchor lighting as the current entry includes it. Found words collect in a tidy column with points. The twin centre distinguishes it from single-centre honeycomb games.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"D — Split-core flower. A + M become one gold object at the centre of a fixed-size seven-cell flower, which solves the 'twin' rule and the stretching at once. Use B's six-slot ledger for the collection.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Twin cells","summary":"A compact 3·2·3 comb in which the two gold cells touch at the centre. The entry bar has A and M sockets that fill as you type. Cells are a fixed 76px (68px under 360px) and never stretch.","layout":"Single column (max 520px): 2/6 progress pips, entry bar with sockets, the comb, action row, collection, hint/reveal.","interaction":"Tap cells (typing is proposed, not wired). The rule line updates live (letters to go, still needs A + M, points if accepted).","motion":"Cells lift on hover and compress on tap. The sockets fill gold.","accessibility":"Gold cells are labelled 'required'. The focus state replaces the outline that clip-path would cut off.","sharing":"Words found out of 6 and points, with no words.","tradeoff":"Other letters sit around the gold pair unevenly, as the 3·2·3 shape requires.","mockup":"twinhive-A.html","path":"team-02/twinhive-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Six-slot ledger","summary":"The goal becomes a numbered six-slot ledger with points per word. Input is a fixed 4×2 grid of 64px keycaps, gold first, with an underlined field and four live check chips.","layout":"Desktop: rack and field on the left, ledger on the right. Phone: stacked.","interaction":"Tap keys or type. Chips update: 4+ letters, has A, has M, hive letters only, and '+n if in dictionary'.","motion":"A new word would slide into the next ledger slot.","accessibility":"Chips use ✓ plus text. The ledger is an ordered list.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"The least 'hive' of the four. Square keys lose the honeycomb identity.","mockup":"twinhive-B.html","path":"team-02/twinhive-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"The collection is the comb","summary":"Each accepted word becomes a physical cell in a six-cell comb, so the goal and the collection are one object. Letters are round keycaps, gold first.","layout":"Desktop: comb on the left, entry and keys on the right. Phone: comb on top.","interaction":"Tap keys. 'Preview fill animation' drops the current entry into the next cell locally, labelled as unchecked.","motion":"The cell springs in from 40% scale.","accessibility":"Cells contain text words. The comb has an aria-label naming the six-word goal.","sharing":"A six-cell outline filled with no words.","tradeoff":"Long words (8+) get tight inside a hex. Found words beyond six need overflow treatment.","mockup":"twinhive-C.html","path":"team-02/twinhive-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Split-core flower","summary":"A classic seven-cell flower whose centre is a single gold hexagon split into A and M halves. The twin requirement is one object. Ticks under the entry preview points: ink for the first three letters, gold per scoring letter.","layout":"Desktop: flower on the left, entry, ticks, actions and collection on the right. Phone: stacked. Cells are a fixed 88px (80px under 360px).","interaction":"Tap petals or either half of the core. Typing is proposed but not wired in the prototype.","motion":"Ticks fill as you type. The core would pulse once when both halves are used.","accessibility":"Each half is at least 40×92px with its own aria-label. Gold letters are also underlined in the entry.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"The split core is a novel affordance; first-time players may tap the middle expecting one letter.","mockup":"twinhive-D.html","path":"team-02/twinhive-D.html"}]},{"id":"deepseek-spare","name":"Spare","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"James called the original too easy and then approved a harder contextual sample. The live harder preview has four context tabs, only one visible context sentence ('A porter lifts a suitcase.'), two slots with ↔, and a dark navy bank of nine words (SHARP, DARK, LIGHT, BRIGHT, FLAT, LOUD, HEAVY, DULL, SOFT). The tabs hide three of four contexts, and the dark bank breaks the light frame. Earlier feedback suggests onboarding confusion.","clarityRisk":"High. 'The same word takes a different opposite depending on context' is subtle.","review":{"slug":"deepseek-spare","name":"Spare","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Harder preview: four context tabs (0/2 placed each), one visible context sentence ('A porter lifts a suitcase.'), two slots with ↔, and a dark navy bank of nine words (SHARP, DARK, LIGHT, BRIGHT, FLAT, LOUD, HEAVY, DULL, SOFT). Tabs hide three of the four contexts, and the dark bank breaks the light frame.","clarityRisk":"High. 'The same word takes a different opposite depending on context' is subtle, and hiding contexts behind tabs makes it harder to see.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Show all four contexts at once, each with a two-slot balance, and a light shared bank below. A placed word appears inside its sentence ('a LIGHT suitcase'), and the spare is one visible slot at the end.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A — Opposite Axes. A follow-up should show all four contexts at once, as the director suggested; this mockup still shows one at a time, because sentences 2–4 were not in the supplied source.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Opposite Axes","summary":"A one-line lead explains the task. A row of four context selectors shows '1 / 0/2'. The active context sentence is set large in serif above an axis: two end slots joined by a double-headed arrow bar. A light three-column bank sits below, then a 'spare' bay that fills itself once eight words are placed, and a four-dot check counter.","layout":"Single column (max 820px). On desktop the spare bay and check history sit side by side; on phones they stack. Return word / Clarify this context / Check all four sit at the bottom.","interaction":"Tap an end slot, then a bank word. Placing a word that is already used elsewhere swaps it in, and the focus moves to the next empty slot. Contexts 2–4 show a 'not in supplied source' placeholder. Check enables at eight placed words (message only). Clarify shows a message only.","motion":"None beyond state changes.","accessibility":"End slots speak 'Context 1, end 2: empty'. Placed words are dimmed and labelled. Check dots have an aria-label. The bank is light, replacing the dark navy panel.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: checks used out of four, Clarify notes used, solved or not, no words.","tradeoff":"Fixes the dark bank and makes 'opposite' a physical axis. But only one context is visible at a time. Check feedback ('how many pairs are right, not which') and Clarify are the prototype's proposals; I could not confirm them in the supplied source in this pass.","mockup":"deepseek-spare-A.html","path":"team-01/deepseek-spare-A.html"}]},{"id":"deepseek-rung","name":"Rung","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"Positive but brief feedback. The live game has a START REST → TARGET SOFT panel, 'Shortest route: 4 steps', current-word tiles, a text field and Undo/Restart/Add rung. It is legible, but it duplicates Morph's mechanic with a weaker presentation, and the shortest-route goal is a sentence rather than something you can see.","clarityRisk":"Low.","review":{"slug":"deepseek-rung","name":"Rung","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"START REST → TARGET SOFT panel, 'Shortest route: 4 steps', current-word tiles, a text field ('Change one letter in REST') and Undo rung/Restart ladder/Add rung. Legible, but it duplicates Morph's mechanic with a different, weaker presentation.","clarityRisk":"Low.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Lean into par: show a fixed ladder with four empty rungs between START and TARGET so each step visibly uses a rung, and going over par extends the ladder in a secondary tone. Consider sharing Morph's ladder component for cohesion.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A — The Climb. It makes the shortest route visible as empty rungs, and it should share Morph's ladder component if both games stay.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"The Climb","summary":"A literal ladder built bottom-up between two rails. REST is an ink START rung at the bottom and SOFT a dashed TARGET rung at the top. Empty dashed rungs show how many steps remain on the shortest route. Changed letters are tinted blue, and letters already matching the target get a green underline.","layout":"On desktop the 400px ladder sits beside a 340px panel (steps so far / shortest route / letters in place, a candidate word with Add rung, an A–Z grid, Undo rung / Restart / Hint). On phones it stacks.","interaction":"Tap a letter in the NOW rung, then a replacement letter (grid or keyboard). The candidate shows with the change in blue. Add rung checks a small demo word list only, and the prototype says so. ←/→ move between letters and Enter adds.","motion":"The selected letter lifts 3px. New rungs appear at the top of the stack.","accessibility":"Letters on the active rung are buttons labelled 'Change letter 1, L'. The step counters are text, not only the rung visuals. Letter keys, arrows and Enter are wired.","sharing":"Not shown. Proposed: steps vs shortest, hints, no words.","tradeoff":"Turns par into a visible ladder. The green 'letters in place' underline is a mild assist. Going over par needs a defined treatment that is not designed here (the director suggested extending the ladder in a secondary tone).","mockup":"deepseek-rung-A.html","path":"team-01/deepseek-rung-A.html"}]},{"id":"deepseek-cipher","name":"Cipher","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"James called it great. The UI is busy: the alphabet keyboard sits above the phrase, and each coded letter is a framed mini-tile inside a framed word group inside a framed panel. Selecting a code doesn't highlight its other occurrences strongly, and a large empty area sits under the phrase.","clarityRisk":"Low to medium. The rules are clear, but tracking which real letter is held by which code is hard.","review":{"slug":"deepseek-cipher","name":"Cipher","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Clue line, then the A–Z selector above the puzzle, then the phrase as small code tiles (guess above, code letter below in ~11px mono), with two givens in green. On phone about 300px of empty white space sits under the phrase. The puzzle text is the smallest text on the screen.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The 'I → ?' selected-code state is good, but repeats of a code are hard to scan at that size.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Phrase first and large: guess letters at 26px and code letters at 15px. Selecting a code highlights every instance in the phrase. Dock the keyboard at the bottom on phone and remove the dead space.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A — Typeset message.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Typeset message","summary":"The phrase is set like type: big guess letters over small code letters, with real word spacing and no tile frames. Selecting a code tints every occurrence. A side panel shows code → answer, how often the code appears, and an alphabet whose used keys show which code holds them.","layout":"Desktop: clue and message on the left, sticky assignment panel on the right. Phone: message first, then a panel with 46px auto-fill keys.","interaction":"Tap a code or use ←/→. Type or tap a letter to assign it. A letter already held by another code is refused until erased (the supplied rule). Live locally.","motion":"The selection tint sweeps across all occurrences.","accessibility":"Each code button speaks its code and guess. Givens are labelled and disabled. The keyboard path is complete.","sharing":"Solved, hints used, letters given, never the phrase.","tradeoff":"On a 320px phone the 7-letter final word fills the row exactly. Longer phrases would need word-level wrapping, which is already allowed.","mockup":"cipher-A.html","path":"team-02/cipher-A.html"}]},{"id":"deepseek-trail","name":"Trail","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The harder 4×4 is the right puzzle, but it sits in a generic rounded-card shell: a small clue, nine faint dot slots, sixteen identical white tiles and a path line that hides under the tiles. Nothing tells you which tiles you can reach next, so the diagonal-adjacency and backtrack rules live only in the collapsed How to play. Check trail sits disabled with no hint that it needs all nine letters.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Diagonal steps, tap-an-earlier-tile-to-cut-back, and 'no strike limit, repeats are free' are invisible during play. The definition button is an underlined link and its help cost is easy to miss.","review":{"slug":"deepseek-trail","name":"Trail","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Harder 4×4 preview: clue headline ('The moment ends. Something stays.'), nine dot slots, a 4×4 grid of raised tiles, Undo/Clear/Check trail. Clean and legible, but generic, with no visible path language on the board.","clarityRisk":"Low-medium. Adjacency (including diagonals or not) isn't shown on the board itself.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Draw the trail as a continuous line through tile centres with step numbers, and give reachable next tiles a subtle rim. The nine slots fill as the path grows, and the clue stays the headline. The hub description ('tiny grid') should follow the shipped 4×4 variant.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Survey plate","summary":"The clue becomes the headline. A nine-slot readout sits under it, and the board is a single plate. Legal next tiles get a dashed ring, unreachable tiles dim, and the route is a thick line drawn across tile centres.","layout":"Phone: clue, readout, rule line, plate, actions, stacked. Desktop: clue, readout and rule on the left; plate and actions on the right.","interaction":"Tap or keyboard (arrows move, Space/Enter select, Backspace undo, Esc clear). Tapping an earlier tile cuts the trail back. Check enables only at 9/9. The definition sits beside the clue, labelled 'counts as help'.","motion":"150 ms tile press and dim transitions. The route line redraws per step. Nothing decorative.","accessibility":"Tiles are 60–90 px. Each tile's label includes row, column, step number and 'legal next step'. The readout is a live region. Reachability uses a dashed outline as well as dimming, not colour alone.","sharing":"Trail 4×4 · solved in [n] checks · definition used/not used. No letters or route.","tradeoff":"Highlighting legal neighbours removes some adjacency bookkeeping. Difficulty stays in finding the word, which is where the feedback put it. It could be made a setting if James wants it harder.","mockup":"trail-A.html","path":"team-03/trail-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Ink trace","summary":"No tile boxes. Letters sit as large discs on a dot lattice and the trail is a thick ink stroke. You can drag through letters in one gesture. The word builds in large type directly under a quoted clue strip.","layout":"Single centred column, max 520 px. Clue strip with a rule above, then the word, then the lattice. A sticky bottom dock holds Undo / Clear / Check.","interaction":"Drag (pointer events) or tap. Lifting and tapping an earlier letter cuts back. Keyboard is the same as A. Check shows how many letters are still needed.","motion":"The newest letter scales slightly and the newest word letter is tinted. The stroke follows your finger.","accessibility":"Every cell is a full 1/4-board hit area even though the disc is smaller. Drag is optional and tap-only works. The dock keeps actions in thumb reach.","sharing":"Same spoiler-free line as A.","tradeoff":"Most tactile and modern, but drag can mis-select diagonals on small phones. It also needs touch-action:none on the board, so the page can't be scrolled from on top of the board.","mockup":"trail-B.html","path":"team-03/trail-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Chart and ledger","summary":"The grid becomes a chart with A–D / 1–4 coordinates. A route ledger lists each step's letter and coordinate, the checked-word history (no strike limit, repeats free), the definition help and keyboard keys.","layout":"Desktop: chart beside the ledger. Phone: chart first, ledger below as a bordered panel.","interaction":"Same selection rules. An illegal tap gets a precise message ('C3 does not touch A2'). The ledger fills as you go.","motion":"Minimal: the route's head dot moves.","accessibility":"Strongest for screen readers and keyboard users: coordinates in every label and an explicit history.","sharing":"Spoiler-free line, plus the check count from the ledger.","tradeoff":"Precise and adult, but less playful. The ledger adds reading load on phones.","mockup":"trail-C.html","path":"team-03/trail-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Keyed instrument","summary":"A single warm-white instrument card. A paper-toned clue window shows the clue (DM Serif) and the nine-cell readout. Below it, sixteen subtle keycaps depress when used; pressed keys turn charcoal and reachable keys get a green base edge.","layout":"Centred device up to 480 px wide. Window, keybed, controls, legend. Definition and Reveal sit outside the device.","interaction":"Press keys or use the keyboard. Pressed keys stay down with step numbers. Undo / Clear / Check are large 52 px controls.","motion":"80 ms key travel. Pressed keys sink by 4 px.","accessibility":"High contrast (warm white on charcoal for pressed keys). The legend explains all three key states in words.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"The most tactile option. Revision 2 removed the dark navy window and metallic key gradients so it sits in the home paper theme; the keycap depth is now a 3 px flat edge. It is still the most object-like of the four.","mockup":"trail-D.html","path":"team-03/trail-D.html"}]},{"id":"deepseek-split","name":"Split","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The harder sample works, but the screen is two stacked white cards and a sixteen-tile grid in one more card, all at the same weight. The active row is shown by a thin outline and the word 'Selected'. That 'four tiles stay behind' and that a check reports how many fit (not which) appear only in the intro and the collapsed rules.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Players may think every tile must be placed, or that check feedback names a row. Up/Down row switching is undiscoverable.","review":{"slug":"deepseek-split","name":"Split","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Two clue cards (six letters each, 'Need a definition?'), a 16-tile rack, and Undo/Clear/Check both. Readable. The active row is shown only by an outline. The hub description ('Divide ten letters between two clues') no longer matches the harder 16-tile, four-decoy keeper.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Which row receives the next tile, and the fact that four tiles must be left over, both need to be obvious.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Two slot rows face each other across the rack, plus a four-slot 'left behind' tray, so the decoys become a visible goal. Tapped tiles fly to the active row. Update the hub description when the variant ships.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"The fork","summary":"Word 1 above the rack, Word 2 below it. An arrow on the rack edge points toward the row that is filling, so Up/Down on the keyboard matches the screen. A counter always says '4 stay behind'.","layout":"One column, max 640 px. Clue 1 and slots, then the rack (4×4 on phones, 8×2 from 620 px), then slots and clue 2, then status and actions.","interaction":"Tap a row or press ↑/↓ to aim. Tap or type rack letters. Tap a filled slot to return it. When a row fills, letters flow to the other row (as the engine does). Check enables at 12.","motion":"The arrow indicator switches ends. Slot underlines turn ink as they fill.","accessibility":"Rack tiles are at least 48 px. Slots are labelled with word and position. Up/Down is spatially honest.","sharing":"Split · both words in [n] checks · [d] definitions used. No letters.","tradeoff":"Clue 2 sits below its slots, which reads slightly backwards. That is the price of the symmetric fork.","mockup":"split-A.html","path":"team-03/split-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Facing pages","summary":"The two clues are two pages of one spread, numbered in large serif figures. On desktop both pages are equal. On phones the page you are not filling folds to a compact strip, so the active clue stays big. The rack is a docked tray labelled '→ Letters go to Word n'.","layout":"Spread (2 columns ≥760 px, stacked below that), then a dock tray with the rack and actions.","interaction":"Tap a page to aim. Arrows switch pages. Typing and slot return work as in A.","motion":"A top accent bar moves to the active page. The folded page shrinks its type.","accessibility":"The folded page keeps 44 px tap height and visible letters. Slots fill with solid ink for high contrast.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Clean and editorial, but on phones you can't see both clues at full size at once.","mockup":"split-B.html","path":"team-03/split-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Sorting tray","summary":"A recessed tray with three grooves: Word 1, Word 2 and an optional Set aside shelf for suspected decoys. Rack tiles are raised blocks.","layout":"Clues in two bordered rows (side by side on desktop), then the tray with three lanes, then the block rack.","interaction":"Choose a lane (tap or ↑/↓), then tap or type letters. The shelf is never checked and costs nothing. It is a UI-only planning aid, not a rule change.","motion":"Blocks lift on hover. Lanes highlight when active.","accessibility":"Lanes are labelled. The shelf is explicitly marked 'not checked'.","sharing":"Same as A. The shelf is not mentioned.","tradeoff":"Adds a planning surface the current game lacks. Treat it as an optional experiment, separate from the core restyle.","mockup":"split-C.html","path":"team-03/split-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"One line, one cut","summary":"Both answers share one twelve-slot beam with a CUT mark in the middle, echoing the name. A check log records each attempt with 'n of 2 fit' dots, never naming the side.","layout":"Clue headers left and right, the beam, the rack, then actions and the check log (side by side on desktop). Under 620 px the beam wraps to 6 | cut | 6.","interaction":"←/→ (or ↑/↓) choose a side. Typing and returning letters work as in A. The log grows with each check.","motion":"Next-slot highlight only.","accessibility":"The log gives persistent, readable feedback instead of a one-off status line.","sharing":"Uses the log: Split · checks [n] · final 2/2.","tradeoff":"The joint-check log makes reasoning easier, which slightly softens the difficulty James liked. It also needs the engine to keep attempt history (not confirmed in the supplied snippet).","mockup":"split-D.html","path":"team-03/split-D.html"}]},{"id":"qwen-quadra","name":"Quadra","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"James likes the category. It is the best idea in the game, but it is set at the same weight as the card around it. The screen stacks four separate surfaces: category card, dotted found-words row, tile grid and a duplicate text input. The 'four chances' pill is tiny. 'A real word can still miss' only appears in help.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The text input and the tile selection can disagree in a player's head, and players may not realise a valid kitchen verb can still cost a chance.","review":{"slug":"qwen-quadra","name":"Quadra","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Category headline ('Kitchen verbs'), four dashed answer slots, a 4×4 tile grid, entry field, Delete/Clear/Lock word. Legible. The dashed slots read as decoration, and the square grid suggests adjacency matters, which it doesn't.","clarityRisk":"Low-medium. The grid shape could imply Boggle-style adjacency.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Category as hero, and tiles as a loose two-row rack rather than a grid. Locked words seat into four engraved slots and their tiles leave the rack, so the shrinking rack is the progress meter.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Category masthead","summary":"The category is set huge as the page masthead. Four answer lanes sit under it and the current word builds directly in lane 1, with no separate input box. Chances are four solid bars.","layout":"Desktop: masthead and lanes left, tile grid and actions right. Phone: stacked, masthead first.","interaction":"Tap tiles in order or type anywhere. Backspace deletes, Esc clears, Enter locks. Tapping a selected tile removes it.","motion":"Selected tiles rise 2 px and show their order number.","accessibility":"Tiles are 60–90 px. Order numbers sit in labels. Chances are also given as text.","sharing":"Quadra · Kitchen verbs · 4/4 found · [m] misses · [h] hints. Category only, no words.","tradeoff":"Dropping the visible text field may surprise keyboard users at first. A typing hint line covers it.","mockup":"quadra-A.html","path":"team-03/quadra-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Composer and dimples","summary":"Tiles are round, flat warm-white pieces in a paper field. Choosing one lifts it into a large four-slot composer and leaves a dimple where it was. Locked words stay in the field in muted tones, so the board becomes a map.","layout":"Single column: soft-green category card (DM Serif category), found-word placeholders, composer, field, actions.","interaction":"Tap or type to lift letters. Tap a composer letter to send it back. Lock word.","motion":"A 220 ms lift from the field into the composer slot.","accessibility":"The composer shows the word in 30–44 px type. Dimples are aria-hidden and their letters stay readable in the composer.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Strong tactility, now with flat warm-white pieces instead of glossy gradients. The category card is quieter than A's masthead, so the category is less of a hero.","mockup":"quadra-B.html","path":"team-03/quadra-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Letter inventory","summary":"A typing-first layout. Four large mono entry cells, with tiles shown either as A–Z stacks (duplicates as one key with a count) or in board order.","layout":"Category header with a misses readout, entry cells, view toggle, inventory, actions, found row.","interaction":"Type freely, or tap a stack to take the next free copy.","motion":"None beyond caret focus.","accessibility":"Excellent for keyboard users. Counts make duplicate tiles explicit (three E, two A, two I).","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Sorting A–Z changes how the puzzle feels and may make it easier. Offer it as a toggle and keep board order as the default if difficulty matters.","mockup":"quadra-C.html","path":"team-03/quadra-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Type case and ledger","summary":"A paper ledger with four numbered lines and punched miss holes on the margin, over a type case of tall letter sorts. A side column holds attempts, 'what counts' and help.","layout":"Desktop: ledger and case left, 300 px side column right. Phone: side panels follow below.","interaction":"Tap sorts (a raised sort is in the current line) or type. Lock line.","motion":"Sorts rise 10 px when selected.","accessibility":"The ledger lines use 28–36 px type. Each miss hole has a text equivalent.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"The most distinctive material. The attempts list needs history that the snippet does not show is stored.","mockup":"quadra-D.html","path":"team-03/quadra-D.html"}]},{"id":"qwen-logic-lattice","name":"Logic Lattice","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"Parked. The '?' in every square and the numbered border make it read like Minesweeper, which James noticed. Tools, an example, a selected-line summary, a legend, diagonal cards, a highlighted-line panel and a collapsed explanation all compete for attention, and the diagonal clues sit far from their diagonals.","clarityRisk":"High. James explicitly asked for more explanation; 'count the whole line' must be visible at first glance.","review":{"slug":"qwen-logic-lattice","name":"Logic Lattice","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"5×5 grid with row/column counts, two diagonal count cards, Cycle/Black/White/Clear mode buttons, a '?' in every undecided cell, and a large highlighted-line explainer. The '?'-filled grid and number edges recall Minesweeper. Four mode buttons for a two-state cell is heavy.","clarityRisk":"High. Counts cover whole lines, not neighbours.","bestDesignOpportunity":"For later: each line label becomes a five-slot gauge that fills as you mark black squares on that line, so 'counts the whole line' is visible. Undecided cells are blank, and tapping cycles states. Parked: no current effort.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A — Line counters. If James revisits the game, start here.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Line counters","summary":"Undecided squares are plain. Every total becomes a live counter (black so far / needed) that turns green when met and red when impossible. The ↘ total sits at the top-left corner where its diagonal starts, and the ↙ total at the bottom-left where it ends. A one-line lesson shows that '2' means two anywhere.","layout":"One-line lesson strip on top. Desktop: tools and board on the left, selected-line guide on the right. Phone: stacked. Board cells are about 46px at 320px.","interaction":"Pick Black, White or Clear, then tap squares. Tap a total to band its five squares and update the guide (strip, tally, deduction sentence). Live locally from today's totals and givens.","motion":"The band slides between selected lines.","accessibility":"Totals are buttons with full spoken status. Fixed squares carry a diamond mark plus a label. White is a dot, black is solid, and undecided is blank.","sharing":"Solved, hints used.","tradeoff":"Live counters are a mild assist compared with today's guide, which already reports counts. The game stays parked; this is for a later decision.","mockup":"lattice-A.html","path":"team-02/lattice-A.html"}]},{"id":"qwen-syntax-siege","name":"Syntax Siege","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"James loves the gameplay but says the UI needs work. It is a dark navy panel inside the light frame, which breaks cohesion. The enemy diamond doesn't move in practice, which James flagged. Pace text ('Auto pace · tiles 30s / typing 20s') reads like a settings dump.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Time pressure plus a dark, dense HUD makes it harder to see what to do next.","review":{"slug":"qwen-syntax-siege","name":"Syntax Siege","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A dark navy game panel with cyan accents: core integrity 100/100 HP, word power, wave tabs 1–6, a small core-to-diamond lane (60 HP), six letter tiles and a glowing 'Start defense'. It reads as generic sci-fi dashboard (the 'fake neon' the brief bans) and breaks the light frame.","clarityRisk":"Medium. HP, points and the clock compete, and the approach lane is too small to feel the threat.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Bring it into the light frame as a precise instrument: a wide lane where a solid ink chevron advances toward the core at the left, with segmented HP under it. Big raised letter tiles, and fired words visibly travel along the lane and chip the enemy. Tension comes from motion and spacing, not glow. Keep input-based pacing and moving practice enemies.","priority":"high"},"recommended":"A — Light firing range.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Light firing range","summary":"Keeps the tension on the home page's warm paper: a warm-white range with a flat green core marker on the left and a charcoal faceted enemy diamond (red outline) that physically travels the lane, including a looping approach in untimed practice. A plain HUD shows core HP, word power and six wave chips.","layout":"Single column (max 720px): HUD, wave chips, range lane, pace line with clock, time bar, feedback line, word tray, six 44px+ rack tiles, Undo/Clear/Fire, Start, and assist and stats.","interaction":"Start begins the local motion preview. Tap tiles or type. The pace label switches to 'Keyboard detected · 20 s' on the first typed key. Fire shows a would-deal preview without checking the word. Damage, waves, HP, the clock countdown and the untimed assist are not wired.","motion":"The enemy approaches over 30s and the time bar drains in sync. A hit would knock the enemy back with a small HP tick.","accessibility":"The range has an aria-label with enemy HP. Feedback is aria-live. Reduced-motion users get a static distance meter instead of travel.","sharing":"Waves cleared, core HP left, points, impacts and whether untimed assist was used.","tradeoff":"Less dramatic than a dark arena. Motion must stay smooth on low-end phones.","mockup":"siege-A.html","path":"team-02/siege-A.html"}]},{"id":"qwen-memory-maze","name":"Memory Maze","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"The mechanic is clear, but the timer is a page element. James scrolled past it and asked for a countdown he can see off to the side. The 7×7 board is a generic grid of rounded boxes, and blocked squares appear with no visual weight.","clarityRisk":"High for timing. The study countdown and recall clock must stay visible whenever the board is visible.","review":{"slug":"qwen-memory-maze","name":"Memory Maze","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A 7×7 pale grid with S and a diamond finish, 'Ready to remember? 8 squares · six seconds to study', a Study button and three stat tiles. The grid is 49 identical outlined boxes, which is flat and generic, and the timer can still be separated from the board by scrolling.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The route and the countdown must be visible together during study.","bestDesignOpportunity":"A countdown ring that drains around the board's perimeter over the six seconds, so the timer can't be scrolled away from the route. Cells are raised studs; during study the route shows as numbered pressed-in studs, and retracing presses studs down.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Sticky instrument bar","summary":"A pinned bar under the header shows the phase (Study / Recall / Done), a draining track, a large seconds readout, and squares, misses and replays. It never scrolls away.","layout":"Sticky bar full width. Board, actions and hint follow in a 560 px column.","interaction":"Study the route runs the real 6 s countdown in the prototype, then recall on an illustrative route. A wrong tap adds +2 s and keeps the trail. The hint replays the route.","motion":"The track drains linearly. A wrong cell shakes for 350 ms.","accessibility":"role=timer. The phase is spelled out as text. Narrow phones show only the active phase label.","sharing":"Memory Maze · 8 squares · [t]s incl. penalties · [m] misses · [r] replays. No route.","tradeoff":"A small amount of header height is always used. This answers the feedback most directly.","mockup":"maze-A.html","path":"team-03/maze-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Floor plan with side rail","summary":"The board is drawn as an architectural plan: walls are solid ink, the start is a ring and the finish a diamond. On desktop a vertical timer rail sits beside the board. On phones it becomes a fixed bottom dock.","layout":"Board plus a 150 px rail on desktop. On phones, board plus a fixed dock.","interaction":"Same as A.","motion":"The rail and dock drain over one-second steps.","accessibility":"The dock respects safe-area insets. Walls are announced as 'wall'.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"This is literally 'off to the side', as requested. The fixed dock covers about 80 px at the bottom of a phone.","mockup":"maze-B.html","path":"team-03/maze-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Perimeter clock","summary":"The board's own frame is the timer. While studying, its edge drains clockwise. While recalling, it fills with squares retraced. Cells are round pegs.","layout":"Caption and big readout above the framed board. Legend and actions below.","interaction":"Same as A.","motion":"The frame stroke animates over one-second steps.","accessibility":"The numeric readout duplicates the frame. Cells keep a full-cell hit area of about 40 px at 320 px wide.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Elegant and impossible to scroll away from the board. One frame carrying two meanings needs the legend line.","mockup":"maze-C.html","path":"team-03/maze-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Chart and step tape","summary":"Graph-paper board with A–G / 1–7 coordinates. A sticky step tape logs each retraced square by coordinate. Misses float a '+2s' tag from the tapped cell.","layout":"Sticky tape with the clock, prompt ('Which square is step 4?'), charted board, actions.","interaction":"Same as A. Arrow-key focus skips disabled cells.","motion":"The +2s tag rises and fades over 1.2 s.","accessibility":"Coordinates in labels and messages ('C3 is not step 4').","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Coordinates help memorisation for some players, which may make it slightly easier.","mockup":"maze-D.html","path":"team-03/maze-D.html"}]},{"id":"qwen-pattern-prism","name":"Pattern Prism","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"Rejected repeatedly. Satin triangles with tiny arrows and name captions still read as children's shape cards. Each token carries three lines of caption, so the eye reads words, not patterns. Direction is ambiguous for circles, and there is no tool for the actual task: separating four independent cycles.","clarityRisk":"High. The puzzle is four interleaved cycles, but the layout presents one row of mixed objects.","review":{"slug":"qwen-pattern-prism","name":"Pattern Prism","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Five example cards with shaded triangle/square/circle forms in sage, rose and gold, each captioned with shape · colour · size · direction, then 06–08 slots and four A–D option cards. Pastel textbook shapes read as educational and childlike. Needing a text label on every attribute shows the visuals don't carry the information.","clarityRisk":"High. Four simultaneous cycles, and the visuals rely on captions.","bestDesignOpportunity":"A rethink, not a recolour: an instrument-grade sequence machine. Each step is a machined puck with one physical indicator per attribute: a notch angle (direction), engraved slot count 1–4 (shape cycle), fill depth (size) and one accent ring from blue/teal/gold/ink (colour). Pucks sit on a rail, and the player builds the next puck by turning the notch and setting the fill, like dialling a combination, which is more fun and adult than multiple choice. Keep the cycle logic. Optional, separate: a timed matrix bonus round.","priority":"urgent"},"recommended":"A — Signal decoder. A rethink, not a recolour.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Signal decoder","summary":"The pieces become flat, matte pucks (one solid signal colour, a printed warm-white shape and a rim notch for direction), seated in shallow wells on one warm-white sequence rail (01–08). A decoder table splits Shape, Colour, Size and Direction into rows so each cycle becomes readable. Choices A–D are pucks you preview in slot 06, then lock in.","layout":"The rail spans the top. Desktop: decoder on the left, choices on the right. Phone: stacked.","interaction":"Select a choice (or press A–D) to preview it seated in 06, then lock it in (local, no right/wrong shown). An optional experiment, off by default, adds per-row 'repeats every 1–4' controls that project the player's own guess into the 06 column.","motion":"The chosen puck slides into its well. The rim notch makes rotation visible.","accessibility":"Every puck has a full aria-label. Colour is backed by named rows in the decoder. The direction notch works for circles.","sharing":"Steps solved, misses and hints, with an optional row of three blank pucks.","tradeoff":"The decoder makes the IQ-test nature explicit and may lower difficulty. The cycle-tuning experiment lowers it further, so it is kept separate and off for Daily.","mockup":"prism-A.html","path":"team-02/prism-A.html"}]},{"id":"qwen-territory-tactics","name":"Territory Tactics","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"This is the current 37-hex, three-placement, exact-capture edition with 240 boards. The rule text is good but long. The board is small on phones with sideways swiping. Groups and their openings aren't shown on the board, so players must count by eye — the source of 'confusing even though I'm getting past levels'.","clarityRisk":"High. Counting groups and openings across 37 hexes is the core difficulty, and the UI currently gives no on-board help.","review":{"slug":"qwen-territory-tactics","name":"Territory Tactics","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A 37-hex board of blue stones, amber diamond targets and '+' on every empty hex, a 'CAPTURE EXACTLY 3 · NEW STONES USED 0/3' counter, and a three-step 'How to capture a group' explainer with a before/after diagram. The rules are now well taught, but on phone the explainer pushes the board about 1,250px down, and the '+' glyphs add noise. The hub card still says 'Place two stones…', which contradicts the current rule.","clarityRisk":"High. Groups, openings and exact totals must be read off the board.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Teach on the board. Tapping an amber group outlines it, marks its openings with numbered rings and forecasts '+3 if all openings are filled'. The exact counter becomes a balance (captured vs target), with overshoot shown in red. The before/after lesson collapses after the first solve. Keep large hit targets and pan, and fix the hub description.","priority":"high"},"recommended":"A — Survey table.","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Survey table","summary":"The exact target is the headline ('Capture exactly 3 amber — not 2, not 4'), and the three remaining stones are physical tokens. Tapping any amber stone outlines its whole group and marks each opening, with a side readout of group size, openings and the resulting total, flagging overshoot. A three-step before/after lesson stays in context and collapses once learned.","layout":"Goal bar, collapsible lesson, then the board (desktop: board on the left, inspect card, last move and actions on the right; phone: stacked).","interaction":"Tap amber to inspect, tap empty to place. A local rule preview runs captures, the exact-total messages and the 'own group needs an opening' rule. Undo and Restart are free.","motion":"Captured hexes flash amber and clear. The stone tokens empty as you place.","accessibility":"Every hex is a button labelled by row and state. Hexes fit the width at about 35px on a 320px phone; 'Zoom board' sets 52px hexes that pan inside the board frame (not the page). Keyboard: Tab and Enter.","sharing":"Exact target met, stones used, hints, with no board.","tradeoff":"Inspect makes counting much easier, which may make the game feel easier. It shows only facts already visible on the board, so it can stay or be limited. The local capture preview is not the shipped engine.","mockup":"territory-A.html","path":"team-02/territory-A.html"}]},{"id":"claude-dovetail","name":"Dovetail","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"Eight full-width bars with tiny row numbers. The thing you are actually solving, the joined word between rows, is never shown until both rows are solved. Typing happens in a separate input below the board, far from the row it fills.","clarityRisk":"Medium. That each missing word must work both above and below, and what the ↑/↓ guess arrows mean, are only in help.","review":{"slug":"claude-dovetail","name":"Dovetail","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A vertical chain of eight rows: SNAP (top) and HOOD (bottom) on dark slate plates, six white rows with underscores and length numbers, joined by tiny connector ticks, with an input field below. Legible but generic: the joint between words, which is the whole idea, is a 2px tick.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Which two rows form each compound isn't visible.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Make the joint the hero: between rows, a dovetail notch shows the compound once both neighbours exist (SNAP|___ → the joined word), lighting when valid. Rows interlock physically, and the slate end plates become light plates with an ink edge to stay in the frame. Keep six misses and hint costs.","priority":"medium"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Joinery column","summary":"Rows stay stacked, but each gap between rows shows the joined word as you type it (SNAP + your letters). You type straight into the row; a transparent input sits over the cells, so phone keyboards still open.","layout":"560 px column: misses notches, a one-line rule with BOOK+WORM / CAR+PET, then the alternating row and joint stack, then actions.","interaction":"Tap a row, type. Fit word enables at the right length. The joints update live; this is concatenation, not validation.","motion":"None needed. The joint text changes colour when live.","accessibility":"The input is labelled with row and length. Given rows are high contrast.","sharing":"Dovetail · 6/6 placed · [m] of 6 misses ([h] from letter hints).","tradeoff":"The live join preview makes the rule obvious without changing the difficulty.","mockup":"dovetail-A.html","path":"team-03/dovetail-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Seven joins","summary":"The chain is shown as seven compound joins. Each missing word appears twice, as the end of one join and the start of the next. Selecting it highlights both. Halves are linked by a dovetail tab.","layout":"List of seven join rows, then an entry field labelled with both join numbers.","interaction":"Tap any blank half to choose that word. Type; both copies fill.","motion":"The tab colour links the selected word's two places.","accessibility":"Compact sizing below 480 px keeps 5+5-letter joins inside a 320 px screen.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Best at teaching the 'both neighbours' constraint. The repeated words add visual density.","mockup":"dovetail-B.html","path":"team-03/dovetail-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Chain table with keyboard","summary":"A tight ruled table of all eight rows in mono type, a docked on-screen keyboard, and an in-context guess line explaining ↑ (fits above) and ↓ (fits below).","layout":"HUD, table, guess-feedback panel, keyboard.","interaction":"On-screen or physical keys. ↑/↓ change row. 'Fit' is the Enter key.","motion":"Key press travel only.","accessibility":"The keyboard avoids native-keyboard layout jumps. Key widths fall below 44 px at 320 px wide, as on any QWERTY layout, but keys are 50 px tall.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Fast for repeat players. The keyboard takes vertical space.","mockup":"dovetail-C.html","path":"team-03/dovetail-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Spotlight","summary":"Only the row being solved is large. Its two neighbours sit directly above and below, with live joins. A slim map on the left shows the whole chain as letter-length bars.","layout":"Map column (56–90 px) plus the spotlight stack, prev/next buttons and actions.","interaction":"Tap a map bar or use the prev/next buttons. Tap the spotlight to type.","motion":"Optional 200 ms slide between rows (not built in the prototype).","accessibility":"Large type in focus; the map bars are labelled.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Calm on phones, but you lose the at-a-glance view of every solved word.","mockup":"dovetail-D.html","path":"team-03/dovetail-D.html"}]},{"id":"claude-splice","name":"Splice","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"Card tiles with fake connector nubs and a heavy navy instruction bar. The selected pair appears in the bar, but nothing explains why an unfamiliar word (HOUSEMAN, in the feedback) was accepted, or that any complete valid pairing counts.","clarityRisk":"Medium. 'Accepted only if every remaining half still has a partner' is the real rule and it is hidden.","review":{"slug":"claude-splice","name":"Splice","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Twelve half tiles (LESS, COLOR, MAN, SOME, RAIN, WATER, FALL, FRESH, BACK, TIME, HORSE, HOME) with nubs on both sides, a dark instruction banner, and Flip/Splice/Shuffle/Deselect. The symmetrical nubs don't express first half vs second half, and accepted uncommon words appear with no explanation.","clarityRisk":"Medium. Order matters, and valid joins can strand the remaining halves.","bestDesignOpportunity":"A single splice bench with a left (first-half) and right (second-half) slot that previews the joined word before commit. Accepted uncommon words get a one-line definition in the solved list.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Splicing bench","summary":"Two sockets (First / Second) with a Flip between them, and a live readout that shows hovered or focused candidates as a preview before you commit. Halves are angled tape strips. The acceptance rule is stated on the bench, and spliced words carry a 'why accepted' line.","layout":"HUD, bench (sockets, readout, actions, rule), strips grid (2/3/4 columns), tools, spliced-words area.","interaction":"Tap the first half and then the second (or hover/focus to preview). Flip, Splice, Shuffle, Show a safe splice.","motion":"Strips lift on hover. The preview socket tints.","accessibility":"Strip labels include the selection order. Previews also trigger on keyboard focus.","sharing":"Splice · 6/6 words · [m] of 4 mistakes · [h] hints.","tradeoff":"The 'why accepted' line needs the engine to expose the reason. The supplied snippet only shows accept/reject messages.","mockup":"splice-A.html","path":"team-03/splice-A.html"}]},{"id":"claude-sprout","name":"Sprout","kind":"keeper","expected":4,"diagnosis":"Every version so far keeps the words separate from the plant. The current one stacks letter rows on top of a generic leaf-and-pot illustration, so the plant is a backdrop, not the record of play. Previous letter-leaf and word-vine attempts were rejected as looking bad. The plant must be built from the accepted words.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The rules (use every letter, add exactly one soil letter, wilts for listed dead-end words) are clear in the rack labels. The visual promise is what fails.","review":{"slug":"claude-sprout","name":"Sprout","keeper":true,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A small leaf-and-pot illustration with an empty word box and an 'A L L' tile card pasted over the leaves, a 3→8 step track, separate 'Your word' and 'Soil' panels, then the entry field and controls. The answers are cards overlapping the plant, which is exactly the separation James rejected. The plant does not change with play.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The rules (keep every letter, add exactly one soil letter, rearrange, wilts) are long paragraphs below.","bestDesignOpportunity":"The word IS the plant. Each accepted word becomes a new stem segment with its letters set along it as nodes, extending upward with visible motion. The added soil letter unfurls as a leaf bud at its node. The eight-letter bloom forms a crown whose petals are the final word's letters. Soil letters wait as seeds in a soil bed under the stem and rise into place when used. Wilts appear as drooping leaves on the stem, not a counter. No history chips or cards anywhere.","priority":"urgent"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Espalier","summary":"A tree trained on precision trellis wires. Each accepted word is a horizontal tier: a branch made of letter segments, centred on the trunk, with leaves at its tips. The letter added from the soil is a fresh pale segment. The next word is a dashed branch on the next wire that fills as you pick letters.","layout":"Desktop: plant left, rack and controls right. Phone: plant, then 'use every letter of RALLY' tiles, soil pebbles, Grow and recovery.","interaction":"Tap or type. Letters fill the dashed branch on the plant itself. Grow / Delete / Clear / Undo growth / Restart / Which letter? (1 wilt) are kept.","motion":"On acceptance the branch extends from the trunk outward (700 ms), segments settle in sequence and leaves unfurl. 'Replay last growth' demonstrates this.","accessibility":"Horizontal words keep normal reading order. The SVG has a full text description, the rack tiles are 48×52 px, and motion respects reduced-motion settings.","sharing":"Sprout · bloomed at 8 letters · [w] of 5 wilts ([h] hints). Optionally a tiny letterless silhouette of tier lengths.","tradeoff":"The most readable integration. It is structurally related to the rejected 'letter branches', but symmetric, wire-guided and solid rather than organic and scattered. James should judge it against those directly.","mockup":"sprout-A.html","path":"team-03/sprout-A.html"},{"id":"B","title":"Rosette","summary":"A succulent seen from above. Each word is a ring of thick leaves, read clockwise from a marker at 12 o'clock. The seed is at the centre and each accepted word adds an outer ring. The soil letter's leaf is pale.","layout":"Circular bed (max 520 px), legend, then the rack panel. Two columns on desktop.","interaction":"Picked letters fill the dashed leaves of the next ring in order.","motion":"A new ring's leaves rotate open from the centre in a staggered sequence.","accessibility":"Letters stay upright. Inner letters are about 17 px at 320 px wide, the weakest readability of the four. The SVG description lists every word.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"The most striking and least like a word list. Reading words around a circle is harder.","mockup":"sprout-B.html","path":"team-03/sprout-B.html"},{"id":"C","title":"Blade fan","summary":"Upright leaf blades rise from a planter. Each blade is one word, read tip to base, and longer words make taller blades. New blades rise from the centre, so the fan builds toward a tall middle. Faint outlines show where blades 7 and 8 will grow.","layout":"Scene panel, then the RALLY tiles and soil tray (side by side above 600 px), then controls.","interaction":"Picked letters fill the dashed blade from its tip.","motion":"A new blade rises with scaleY from its base (800 ms).","accessibility":"Vertical stacking keeps every letter upright. The legend explains the reading direction.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"Sculptural and calm. Reading top-to-bottom is slower than horizontal.","mockup":"sprout-C.html","path":"team-03/sprout-C.html"},{"id":"D","title":"Trailing planter","summary":"A hanging planter whose soil is the soil rack: pebble letters in the pot. Each accepted word trails down as a vine of letter nodes with leaves between them. Strands lengthen left to right.","layout":"Hook, then the planter with soil pebbles, then the vines SVG, then a panel with the current-word tiles and controls.","interaction":"Tap pebbles in the pot and the current-word tiles. Letters drop into the dashed vine.","motion":"A new vine unspools downward and its nodes pop in sequence.","accessibility":"Node letters are about 16–20 px. The planter pebbles are 50 px.","sharing":"Same as A.","tradeoff":"The most literal 'growing' feel, and the soil is physically in the pot. It overlaps C's vertical reading, but the composition and input location differ.","mockup":"sprout-D.html","path":"team-03/sprout-D.html"}]},{"id":"claude-tangle","name":"Tangle · threads","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"Beads in a 5-wide grid hide the fact that they form one ordered string. When a valid word such as PEPPY or PREPS is refused, the player isn't told that the leftover letters can't split into 6 + 7. The name also collides with the kept grid-word Tangle.","clarityRisk":"High. The partition rule, bead-order reading and duplicate copies all need in-context feedback.","review":{"slug":"claude-tangle","name":"Tangle (interleaved words)","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Three thread headers (5, 6, 7 letters), 18 numbered beads wrapped into four rows (P A N R P / U E P S E / I P M Y N / A L T), 'Choose the beads of one word', Back/Clear/Pull thread, and a dense rules paragraph. Wrapping the 'string' into rows breaks the left-to-right order rule visually, so the bead numbers have to carry the order.","clarityRisk":"Urgent-level comprehension issue: a valid word costs a knot when it strands the remainder, and nothing on the board explains why.","bestDesignOpportunity":"One continuous string: beads on a single line (scrollable or serpentine with direction arrows). Selected beads lift onto a coloured thread. When a pull strands the rest, show exactly why in place: 'PEPPY is a word, but the 13 beads left can't form a 6 and a 7', with the remainder highlighted. Optional, separate (it changes difficulty): a pre-pull 'Safe / Strands the rest' indicator.","priority":"high"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"One cord, three threads","summary":"Flat warm-white beads hang on a visible cord numbered 1–18, wrapping row to row. Selected beads lift, and the word reads in bead order with the bead numbers shown. A 'what remains' line shows the leftover beads in order and says they must split into 6 + 7. Three outcome cards explain free, blocked-copy and knot results. A demo shows the PEPPY knot message.","layout":"Thread lanes (5/6/7), cord box, selection readout, remaining strip, actions, result, outcome cards.","interaction":"Tap beads in any order, or type to take the next matching bead after the last one. Back / Clear / Pull.","motion":"Beads lift 8 px when selected.","accessibility":"Beads are at least 42 px (6 per row on phones, 9 on desktop). Every bead number is visible and in its label.","sharing":"Tangle · 3/3 threads · [k] of 4 knots · [h] hints.","tradeoff":"The 'these copies block — try other copies' card proposes highlighting alternatives. That needs engine support (it appears to compute allocations, but this isn't confirmed). Product naming should separate this from the grid Tangle.","mockup":"tangle-A.html","path":"team-03/tangle-A.html"}]},{"id":"claude-keystone","name":"Keystone","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"Clue stones are scattered at different heights with '?' placeholders, and the scoring (lower is better, 2 best, 6 perfect day) is a footnote. The player can't see what a guess would form with each clue.","clarityRisk":"Medium-high. Inverse scoring and 'fits the visible clues but may miss a hidden one' confuse people.","review":{"slug":"claude-keystone","name":"Keystone","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Arch tabs 1–3, five clue blocks arranged as an uneven arch (OUT, HOME shown; three '?' hidden), a keystone input, a keyboard, and 'Score 0 · 6 is perfect'. The arch idea is right but the blocks are small and irregular, and golf-style scoring (lower is better) is counter-intuitive.","clarityRisk":"Medium-high. Before/after joining and the scoring direction.","bestDesignOpportunity":"A true arch with an empty keystone slot at the crown. The guess drops into the crown, and each clue block shows its joined compound with tick or cross, so partial fits are visible. State the score as 'clues used' (fewer is better).","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Wedge and joins","summary":"Three arch tabs at the top. A wedge-shaped keystone input with a plain score sentence ('Solve now and this arch scores 2 — the best possible'). Five join rows show each visible clue with your draft both before and after it, live. Hidden rows explain how they reveal.","layout":"Arch tabs, then the wedge and score on the left with join rows on the right (desktop), stacked on phones. On-screen keyboard on phones only.","interaction":"Type or use the keys. Place keystone enables at 3+ letters.","motion":"None required.","accessibility":"Join previews are text. The hidden-clue cost is stated per row.","sharing":"Keystone · arches [a1]/[a2]/[a3] · total [t] (6 is perfect).","tradeoff":"Live before/after joins make guessing faster but don't validate anything.","mockup":"keystone-A.html","path":"team-03/keystone-A.html"}]},{"id":"claude-tumbler","name":"Tumbler","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"The dial pills only repeat 'A–Z · 26 letters'. The six-row grid looks like an ordinary Wordle-style board, so the alphabetical-range idea, which is the game's whole identity, gets lost.","clarityRisk":"Medium. ▲ / ▼ apply per position only, and players may read them as hot/cold.","review":{"slug":"claude-tumbler","name":"Tumbler","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"Five dark dial capsules ('Dial 1 · A–Z · 26 letters'), a ▲ Later ▼ Earlier ● Exact legend, a 6×5 guess grid, an entry field with Turn lock, and a keyboard. The dials are textual ranges rather than dials, and the 6×5 grid again echoes Wordle.","clarityRisk":"Medium.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Real tumbler drums: each position is a vertical alphabet drum whose window narrows after each guess, with out-of-range letters dimming and rolling away. Guesses log as a compact list with ▲▼● per letter, replacing the 6×5 grid.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Range columns","summary":"Five vertical A–Z tracks, each with a green band for the letters still possible and a marker for your last letter with its ▲/▼. Your typed word sits directly under the column it tests. A Fresh / After-1-guess toggle shows the bands narrowing; that state uses an invented hidden word.","layout":"Lock panel of 5 columns, legend, history rows, keyboard.","interaction":"Type or use the on-screen keys. 'Turn' submits (prototype message only).","motion":"Bands animate to their new range (500 ms).","accessibility":"Each column announces its range and count. Glyphs are paired with a written legend.","sharing":"Tumbler · opened in [n]/6 · [h] hints. Arrows grid without letters.","tradeoff":"Tall on phones. The band visual must stay functional, not decorative.","mockup":"tumbler-A.html","path":"team-03/tumbler-A.html"}]},{"id":"nested-tic-tac-toe","name":"Tic-Tac-Toe","kind":"other","expected":1,"diagnosis":"The only dark page in the collection, which breaks the shared light frame. The route rule is explained well, but by splitting overview and detail it asks phone players to scroll between them.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The 'your square picks the AI's board' route needs a live preview, which exists in the source and should be kept.","review":{"slug":"nested-tic-tac-toe","name":"Tic-Tac-Toe","keeper":false,"currentDesignDiagnosis":"A fully dark navy theme with its own 'a.' logo and an 'ALFORD DAILY · THE GAMES COLLECTION' lockup, a nine-board overview of dark mini boards plus a separate 'Play this board' zoom panel. It breaks the shared light frame and the brand lockup. The overview-plus-zoom pattern itself suits phones.","clarityRisk":"Medium. The send-to rule is explained by a banner, not shown as a move consequence.","bestDesignOpportunity":"Bring it into the light frame: one big board with the active mini board raised and the others recessed. Hovering or focusing a small square draws an arrow to the board it will send the opponent to. Use blue × and teal ○.","priority":"low"},"recommended":"A","designs":[{"id":"A","title":"Route table (light frame)","summary":"A paper-toned macro board of warm-white mini boards. Your × is charcoal and the AI's ○ is green. The forced board carries a 'PLAY HERE' tab, and hovering or focusing a square outlines the destination board with 'AI goes here'. Desktop players can play directly on the big board. Phone players tap a board to open the large detail grid, where each empty square is labelled with where it sends the AI.","layout":"Status bar, macro board. A side column holds the detail board, route text, AI level, three rules and sample-state buttons.","interaction":"Phone: zoom-by-selection (no pinch or pan), because mini cells are about 24 px at 320 px wide. Desktop: direct cell play plus the detail panel. The AI level locks after the first move.","motion":"Destination outline on preview. The claim animation from the source is kept (not rebuilt here).","accessibility":"Detail cells are about 80 px. Route text is a live region. The mini cells on phones are display-only.","sharing":"Tic-Tac-Toe · I won / AI won / draw vs Tactical AI · Alford Daily bonus.","tradeoff":"Two ways to play on desktop (direct and detail) could look redundant. Keep the detail panel mainly for keyboard and phone.","mockup":"tictactoe-A.html","path":"team-03/tictactoe-A.html"}]}],"count":91,"expected":91};
